B.akp=s(["Fine-tune the model on the new topic","Merge the model with a knowledge-focused model","Use RAG to retrieve the missing information","Rewrite the system prompt to include the knowledge"],t.s)
B.axH=new A.a7("When a model lacks knowledge about a specific topic but already has the right habits for answering, what is the most cost-effective first step?",B.akp,2,"Missing knowledge calls for retrieval (RAG); fine-tuning is for missing habits, and a plain prompt is for everything else. The ladder of costs means most problems are solved on the first rung.")
B.al2=s(["trains a small low-rank patch while keeping the original weights frozen","averages the original weights with a randomly generated matrix","stores the base model in 4-bit format and trains new weights from scratch","replaces the entire weight matrix with a new one of the same shape"],t.s)
B.ax9=new A.a7("LoRA differs from full fine-tuning in that it...",B.al2,0,"LoRA freezes the original weights and trains a tiny patch made of two thin matrices whose product has the same shape as the big one. QLoRA is the variant that additionally stores the frozen base in 4 bits.")
B.anh=s(["It skips the backpropagation steps after the first epoch","It compresses the training data before loading it","It stores the frozen base model in 4 bits, drastically reducing memory","It trains fewer parameters by using a lower rank"],t.s)
B.axy=new A.a7("What makes QLoRA able to run on a single consumer GPU where full fine-tuning would need a datacenter?",B.anh,2,"QLoRA's key innovation is storing the frozen base in 4 bits, which cut the memory to fine-tune a 65B model from more than 780 GB to under 48 GB. LoRA already reduces trainable parameters, but the 4-bit storage is what makes it fit on one GPU.")
B.adF=s(["A single concatenated paragraph with no structure","A chat template that stacks system, user, and assistant turns around special token markers","The raw conversation text with no formatting","A JSONL file with one row per message"],t.s)
B.awj=new A.a7("What does the model actually see when it processes a conversation?",B.adF,1,"The chat template wraps system, user, and assistant turns around special token markers, and that is the only structure the model ever sees. JSONL is how training examples are stored, not what the model reads at inference.")
B.anj=s(["The sum of all gradient updates during training","The direction from the base model to the centroid of the training data","The difference between fine-tuned weights and the original weights of the same model","The average of all activations across a batch of examples"],t.s)
B.ayt=new A.a7("A task vector is defined as...",B.anj,2,"A task vector is the fine-tuned weights minus the original weights of the same model. It can be added to combine skills or negated to remove a behaviour.")
B.agt=s(["Merge all values, trim the noise, then elect a sign","Elect a sign, trim to the largest changes, then merge all remaining values","Trim to the largest changes, elect one sign per parameter, then merge only the values that agree","Elect a sign, merge values, then trim to the largest changes"],t.s)
B.axh=new A.a7("TIES-merging resolves interference between task vectors in three steps. Which sequence is correct?",B.agt,2,"TIES trims each task vector to its largest changes, elects one sign per parameter, and merges only the values that agree with that sign.")
B.ai3=s(["Subtracting one task vector from the other and keeping only positive values","Replacing each parameter with the median of the two models","Averaging the two task vectors and discarding the result","Randomly dropping a share of the fine-tuning changes and rescaling the rest"],t.s)
B.ayg=new A.a7("DARE handles interference by...",B.ai3,3,"DARE randomly drops a share of the fine-tuning changes and rescales the remaining changes by 1/(1\u2212p). Because fine-tuning changes are tiny, 90% or even 99% can be dropped.")
B.acP=s(["The average difference in internal activations between examples with and without a target behaviour","A retraining step that adjusts the top few layers","A permanent edit to the model's weights that cannot be undone","A new layer inserted between the attention heads"],t.s)
B.ay7=new A.a7("A steering vector applied at inference time is...",B.acP,0,"CAA defines a steering vector as the average difference in the model's internal activations between examples with and without a behaviour. It is added or subtracted at inference, making it easy to take away.")
B.a7I=s(["Control vectors work only on small models; abliteration works on large models","Control vectors require retraining; abliteration does not","Control vectors change the prompt; abliteration changes the data","Control vectors are added at run time and can be removed; abliteration cuts a direction out of the weights permanently"],t.s)
B.avV=new A.a7("What is the key difference between a control vector and abliteration?",B.a7I,3,"A control vector is added at run time and is easy to take away. Abliteration does the same to a direction (like refusal) but cuts it out of the weights for good, which carries costs in quality and safety.")
B.ajS=s(["They must have exactly the same quantization","They must have been trained on the same dataset","They must both use LoRA adapters","They must be fine-tunes of the same base model, with the same architecture and starting weights"],t.s)
B.awp=new A.a7("What must be true before two fine-tuned models can be merged?",B.ajS,3,"Merging combines what each fine-tune changed relative to a shared starting point, so both must come from the same base model; different bases have unrelated weights.")
B.aci=s(["Most of the time goes into the steps before writing (query rewriting, search, reranking, reading passages), so fix those first","A bigger chat model would fix it","The notes need more documents","The answers are too long"],t.s)
B.ayE=new A.a7("A home notes helper took 65 seconds per answer, but only 5-10 seconds of that was the model writing. What does that tell you?",B.aci,0,"About 90% of each answer came before the first word, so turning off the extra query-writing call and reading fewer passages cut the median from 65 s to 20 s.")
B.amL=s(["So every change can be compared fairly: score and speed before vs after","To make the embedding model smaller","Because Open WebUI requires a test set","Because the model needs the questions to learn"],t.s)
B.ayD=new A.a7("Why test a RAG helper with a fixed set of questions with checked answers?",B.amL,0,"The same 25 checked questions showed which changes really helped (0.80 \u2192 0.91 with curated notes) and which cost nothing (0.89 at a third of the time).")
B.agc=s(["The weather is stored inside the model's weights","The user's phone guesses it","The model connects to the internet by itself","The model writes a structured call; the app runs the tool and sends the result back as text"],t.s)
B.axC=new A.a7("When a chatbot uses a weather tool, who actually fetches the weather?",B.agc,3,"The model stops with a tool call (Claude's API calls it tool_use); the app runs the code and returns a tool_result, which the model reads before answering.")
B.abY=s(["How AI apps connect to tool servers: one plug shape for tools, resources and prompts","How GPUs talk to each other","The size of a context window","How models are trained"],t.s)
B.ayl=new A.a7("What does MCP standardize?",B.abY,0,"MCP is a JSON-RPC protocol: the host app keeps one client per server and uses tools/list and tools/call, so a tool server written once works in any MCP app.")
B.ahO=s(["They are switched off most of the time","Many are polysemantic: one neuron fires for several unrelated things","They only store numbers from the tokenizer","Each neuron stores one whole sentence"],t.s)
B.axb=new A.a7("Why are single neurons hard to read inside a language model?",B.ahO,1,"Anthropic found a neuron in a small model that fired for academic citations, English dialogue, HTTP requests and Korean text; sparse autoencoders untangle such mixtures into cleaner features.")
B.agh=s(["A Claude model running on a server near the bridge","Claude with its Golden Gate Bridge feature clamped to 10\xd7 its maximum, so it brought up the bridge everywhere","Claude with a system prompt telling it to act like a bridge","A version of Claude trained only on San Francisco data"],t.s)
B.awu=new A.a7('What was "Golden Gate Claude"?',B.agh,1,"The researchers turned one internal feature up directly; it wasn't a prompt or fine-tuning, and the model's answers kept steering back to the bridge.")
B.aaB=s([B.axH,B.ax9,B.axy,B.awj,B.ayt,B.axh,B.ayg,B.ay7,B.avV,B.awp,B.ayE,B.ayD,B.axC,B.ayl,B.axb,B.awu],t.b)
