B.Gs=new A.a7('The "FineWeb" library that Bit studies from is cleaned-up web text gathered from many snapshot collections. How big is it?',B.ahV,1,"FineWeb holds 15 trillion tokens of cleaned web text drawn from 96 Common Crawl snapshots \u2014 so much reading that, at a normal human pace of 250 words per minute for 8 hours a day, it would take about 250,000 years to get through.")
B.aoY=s(["A reader's eye flicking back to a key word in a sentence to make sure they really get the meaning","A radio scanner that only picks up the one station you want while ignoring the rest","A translator switching languages in the middle of a sentence","A speaker raising their voice so the whole room can hear clearly"],t.s)
B.awH=new A.a7('In the Transformer, "attention" lets each word look at the other words in a sentence and decide which ones matter most. What everyday situation is this most like?',B.aoY,0,"Attention is the model's way of glancing back at important words \u2014 it assigns each word a \"how much to listen to this one\" weight, so it can focus on the parts of the sentence that actually carry the meaning, just like a reader's eye returns to the noun or verb that anchors the thought.")
B.aeA=s(["The loss stays the same no matter how confident the model is","The loss goes up, meaning the model is getting more wrong","The loss flips from negative to positive each time","The loss goes down, meaning the model is getting less wrong"],t.s)
B.awY=new A.a7("The model's \"loss\" score is a wrongness meter. If the model's confidence in the correct answer goes up (its probability rises), what happens to the loss?",B.aeA,3,"Loss is calculated as \u2212log(probability), so as the model's confidence in the right answer climbs toward 1, the wrongness score shrinks toward zero \u2014 think of it as a thermometer that drops as the answer gets better.")
B.agn=s(["A librarian re-shelving books in alphabetical order","A detective working backward from the crime scene to figure out who was responsible","A chef tasting the soup and deciding which spice to add a pinch more of","A teacher erasing the blackboard before the next lesson"],t.s)
B.axr=new A.a7('After the model makes a wrong guess, "backpropagation" works backward through the network to figure out which weights (the tiny adjustment knobs) should be nudged. What everyday situation fits best?',B.agn,1,'Backpropagation is like a detective tracing the chain of cause-and-effect backward from the final mistake, assigning each "suspect" weight a small share of the blame so the model knows exactly which knob to turn next.')
B.aiJ=s(["The ball freezes in place and never moves at all","The hill flattens out so the ball no longer needs to roll","The ball rolls gently and steadily straight to the bottom","The ball bounces over the next hilltop or shoots off the map entirely"],t.s)
B.axZ=new A.a7('Bit rolls a ball downhill on a "loss landscape" (a bumpy hill where the bottom is the best answer). If the learning rate \u2014 the size of each step \u2014 is set way too large, what happens?',B.aiJ,3,'A learning rate that\'s too big is like taking enormous strides downhill \u2014 the ball overshoots the valley, bounces over the next ridge, and can even "explode" into nonsensical values instead of ever settling at the bottom.')
B.act=s(["300 interruptions; about 59% of the unexpected ones were GPU failures","466 interruptions; all of the unexpected ones were network failures","466 interruptions; about 59% of the unexpected ones were GPU failures","466 interruptions; about 90% of the unexpected ones were GPU failures"],t.s)
B.ax6=new A.a7("During a 54-day training run for the Llama 3 405B model, how many times did the run get interrupted, and what share of the unexpected stops were hardware (GPU) problems?",B.act,2,'In those 54 days there were 466 interruptions \u2014 47 planned and 419 unexpected \u2014 and 58.7% of the unexpected stops were GPU problems, which is why training regularly saves a "game" (a checkpoint) to restart from.')
B.aoQ=s(["Exactly 1 token per parameter, no more","About 20 tokens of training data per parameter","About 200 tokens of training data per parameter","Always use the biggest model you can afford, regardless of how much data you feed it"],t.s)
B.awg=new A.a7("Research published in 2022 (Chinchilla) found that the best performance for a given compute budget comes from a specific balance of model size and training data. What's that sweet spot?",B.aoQ,1,"The Chinchilla result showed that a 70-billion-parameter model trained on 1.4 trillion tokens \u2014 right around 20 tokens per parameter \u2014 beat much larger models that were starved of data, proving that balance beats brute size.")
B.afn=s(["A 3.8B model beat a 65B model, about 17\xd7 its size","A 1.3B model (InstructGPT) beat the 175B GPT-3, over 100\xd7 its size","The 175B GPT-3 won easily, because bigger models are always smarter","A 70B model tied with a 405B model, so size made no difference"],t.s)
B.Gy=new A.a7('After "post-training" (the stage where the model learns to be a helpful assistant rather than just a text-completion machine), people were asked to pick the better answer. Which model won, and what was the size gap?',B.afn,1,"With human-feedback training (RLHF), people consistently preferred the helpful, well-shaped answers from a 1.3-billion-parameter InstructGPT over the raw text-completion output of 175-billion-parameter GPT-3 \u2014 a reminder that a smaller model trained to listen to feedback can outshine a much bigger one.")
