B.aax=s(["Figure 1: Side-by-side diagrams of the CBOW and Skip-gram architectures, showing how each handles context words.","Table 4: The head-to-head accuracy comparison where Skip-gram (300 dim, 783M words) hits 53.3 percent total, far ahead of all prior models listed.","Section 4.2 and Table 2: How accuracy climbs as you increase both vector dimensionality and training data, and why you must raise both together."],t.s)
B.aDi=new A.o("distributed representation","A word's meaning stored as a list of numbers, not a single label.")
B.aC9=new A.o("bag-of-words","Using surrounding words without caring about their order.")
B.aD2=new A.o("hierarchical softmax","A shortcut that scores only a few candidate words instead of the entire vocabulary.")
B.aCR=new A.o("cosine distance","A measure of how aligned two vectors are, from 0 to 1.")
B.azJ=new A.o("stochastic gradient descent","Training by nudging weights a tiny bit after each small batch of data.")
B.a9n=s([B.aDi,B.aC9,B.aD2,B.aCR,B.azJ],t.L)
B.acK=s(["They use a smaller vocabulary","They remove the non-linear hidden layer","They only look at one previous word","They skip the output layer entirely"],t.s)
B.axk=new A.a7("What is the main architectural change that makes CBOW and Skip-gram faster than the feedforward NNLM?",B.acK,1,"The paper states that most of the complexity comes from the non-linear hidden layer, and both new models eliminate it.")
B.acz=s(["The model memorises every sentence it has seen","Relationships between words are encoded in the geometry of the vector space","Vector arithmetic is only valid for proper nouns","Words are stored in alphabetical order"],t.s)
B.aw2=new A.a7('What does the equation vector("King") - vector("Man") + vector("Woman") \u2248 vector("Queen") demonstrate?',B.acz,1,"The paper uses this example to show that linear regularities among words are preserved in the learned vector space.")
B.aaL=s(["They required no training data at all","They could only be trained on English text","They produced vectors of fixed 50 dimensions","They made it feasible to train high-quality vectors on billions of words in hours"],t.s)
B.ay2=new A.a7("What was the key practical advantage of the new architectures over previous neural-network approaches?",B.aaL,3,"The abstract highlights that it takes less than a day to learn high-quality vectors from 1.6 billion words, a scale previously unreachable.")
B.ad8=s([B.axk,B.aw2,B.ay2],t.b)
B.aum=new A.cS("SRC-WORD2VEC","word2vec","2013","Tomas Mikolov et al., Google","Words can be turned into lists of numbers (vectors) such that similar words sit close together, and relationships like king-to-man equals queen-to-woman show up as simple arithmetic on those numbers.",'Before this, words were just indices in a vocabulary with no built-in notion of similarity, and the neural-network methods that did learn word vectors were too slow to train on billions of words. Mikolov and colleagues proposed two simpler architectures, CBOW and Skip-gram, that drop the non-linear hidden layer from a neural network language model, cutting the per-word computation dramatically. Trained on 783 million words with 300-dimensional vectors, the Skip-gram model scored 53.3 percent total accuracy on their Semantic-Syntactic Word Relationship test set (50.0 percent semantic, 55.9 percent syntactic). The authors showed that vector("King") minus vector("Man") plus vector("Woman") lands closest to vector("Queen"), and that training on 1.6 billion words takes less than a day. They also beat the previous state of the art on the Microsoft Sentence Completion Challenge by combining Skip-gram scores with RNNLM scores, reaching 58.9 percent.',"It made high-quality word vectors cheap and fast to produce at a scale that was previously impossible, turning them into a standard building block for NLP. The geometric insight that meaning lives in the shape of a vector space became a core idea behind every modern embedding layer.","Every model you run starts by turning tokens into vectors with an embedding layer, the descendant of this idea. Separate embedding models do the same for whole sentences, which is how document search (RAG) in tools like Open WebUI finds the passages closest to your question.","The detailed computational-complexity formulas in Section 2 and the DistBelief distributed-training setup in Section 2.3 and 4.4 are implementation details you can safely skip.",B.aax,B.a9n,B.ad8)
B.alp=s(["Figure 1: The one-picture summary of the whole architecture \u2014 an LSTM reads 'ABC' into a vector, another LSTM writes 'WXYZ' out of it.","Section 3.3 (Reversing the Source Sentences): The short explanation of why flipping the input order made training dramatically easier, with the perplexity and BLEU numbers.","Table 1: The main results table showing how each configuration (single model, ensemble, beam size) stacks up against the SMT baseline."],t.s)
B.aB8=new A.o("LSTM","A neural network that keeps a running memory as it reads a sequence.")
B.aCb=new A.o("Encoder-decoder","One network summarizes the input; a second network writes the output from that summary.")
B.aBj=new A.o("BLEU","A 0-to-100 score measuring how close a machine translation is to a human one.")
B.aBY=new A.o("Beam search","A decoding strategy that keeps a few best guesses and extends them one word at a time.")
B.ayW=new A.o("Out-of-vocabulary (OOV)","A word the model never saw during training, so it cannot produce it.")
B.agS=s([B.aB8,B.aCb,B.aBj,B.aBY,B.ayW],t.L)
B.ajO=s(["A single feedforward network that maps the whole sentence in one step","An LSTM encoder that compresses the source into a fixed vector, and an LSTM decoder that writes the target word by word","A convolutional network that slides a window over the source sentence","A transformer with self-attention over the full sequence"],t.s)
B.awq=new A.a7("What was the core architecture of the model?",B.ajO,1,"The paper uses two LSTMs: one reads the input into a fixed-dimensional vector, the other decodes the output sequence from that vector.")
B.acS=s(["It made the target sentence shorter, reducing the number of decoding steps","It increased the vocabulary coverage by exposing rarer word forms","It reduced the number of parameters the model needed to store","It placed corresponding source and target words close together, creating short-term dependencies that made optimization easier"],t.s)
B.ax2=new A.a7("Why did reversing the source sentence order improve performance so much?",B.acS,3,"Reversing the source brought the first few source words next to the first few target words, cutting the 'time lag' and letting backpropagation learn the mapping more easily.")
B.aog=s(["34.81, beating the SMT baseline of 33.30","36.5, beating the SMT baseline of 33.30","33.30, tying the SMT baseline","37.0, beating the SMT baseline of 33.30"],t.s)
B.awU=new A.a7("What BLEU score did the best LSTM ensemble achieve on WMT'14 English-to-French, and how did it compare to the SMT baseline?",B.aog,0,"The ensemble of 5 reversed LSTMs with beam size 12 scored 34.81, outperforming the phrase-based SMT system's 33.30.")
B.af4=s([B.awq,B.ax2,B.awU],t.b)
B.auL=new A.cS("SRC-SEQ2SEQ","Seq2Seq","2014","Ilya Sutskever et al., Google","Read a sentence into a fixed-size summary with one neural network, then write a new sentence out of that summary with a second one. No hand-crafted alignment needed.","Standard neural networks need fixed-size inputs, so they cannot handle sentences of varying length. Sutskever and colleagues built an encoder-decoder: an LSTM reads the source sentence word by word and compresses it into a single 8,000-dimensional vector, then a second LSTM decodes the target sentence from that vector. On WMT'14 English-to-French, an ensemble of 5 LSTMs (384M parameters) scored 34.81 BLEU, beating the phrase-based SMT baseline of 33.30. A simple trick \u2014 reversing the word order of the source sentence \u2014 dropped test perplexity from 5.8 to 4.7 and raised BLEU from 25.9 to 30.6, because it put corresponding words close together and made the learning problem easier. The model also handled long sentences well, something earlier RNN approaches struggled with.","It showed that a single neural network trained end to end could translate better than a carefully engineered statistical system, with no hand-made alignment rules. Sequence-to-sequence learning became the frame for translation, summarising and, later, chatbots.",'Translation models and encoder-decoder families such as T5 and BART follow this read-then-write layout. Chat models are "decoder-only" (one network reads your prompt and writes the reply), but the idea of turning one sequence into another with a neural network starts here.',"Section 3.5 on parallelization and the C++ implementation details (8 GPUs, 6,300 words per second) are engineering logistics that will not change how you think about the model.",B.alp,B.agS,B.af4)
B.aa0=s(["Figure 2: The one-picture explanation of the residual building block (F(x) + x with the shortcut)","Section 3.1: Why learning a residual is easier than learning the full mapping from scratch","Table 3: Side-by-side error rates showing plain nets degrade with depth while ResNets keep improving"],t.s)
B.aCY=new A.o("shortcut connection","A path that skips layers and adds the input straight to the output.")
B.azK=new A.o("residual function","The small correction a layer learns on top of just passing the input through.")
B.aCN=new A.o("degradation problem","Deeper plain networks actually get worse at training, not just at testing.")
B.azw=new A.o("bottleneck block","A block that shrinks dimensions, does work, then expands back to the original size.")
B.afj=s([B.aCY,B.azK,B.aCN,B.azw],t.L)
B.afi=s(["It reduces the total number of parameters in the network","It forces gradients to flow backward through more layers","The layers only need to learn a small correction rather than the full mapping","It eliminates the need for batch normalization"],t.s)
B.awX=new A.a7("Why does the residual formulation make optimization easier?",B.afi,2,"The paper argues that driving weights toward zero to approximate an identity is easier than fitting an arbitrary function from scratch.")
B.aoI=s(["Passes the input unchanged and adds it to the output of the stacked layers","Applies a learned linear transformation to the input before adding it","Drops the input and lets the stacked layers handle everything","Averages the input with the layer output to reduce noise"],t.s)
B.ayd=new A.a7("What does the identity shortcut connection actually do?",B.aoI,0,"The shortcut performs identity mapping and element-wise addition, adding no extra parameters or computation.")
B.ahZ=s(["Deeper networks always overfit on the training data","Training loss increases with depth even before any test evaluation","The network forgets early layers' information as it gets deeper","Adding layers increases the number of parameters beyond what is available"],t.s)
B.axE=new A.a7("What is the 'degradation problem' the paper addresses?",B.ahZ,1,"The paper shows that plain networks with more layers have higher training error throughout the entire training procedure, not just worse test accuracy.")
B.anW=s([B.awX,B.ayd,B.axE],t.b)
B.auq=new A.cS("SRC-RESNET","ResNet","2015","Kaiming He et al., Microsoft Research","Instead of asking each layer to figure out the full answer from scratch, you let it learn only the small correction on top of the input, and a shortcut connection passes the input through unchanged.","Deep networks were getting harder to train as they grew: adding more layers actually made the training error go up. The authors noticed that a deeper network could always just copy the shallower one and set the extra layers to do nothing, so the problem was optimization, not capacity. Their fix: each block learns a small residual correction F(x) and adds it to the input via a shortcut connection, so the network only has to learn what to change rather than the whole thing. This let them train a 152-layer network that is 8x deeper than VGG yet has lower computational cost. Their ensemble hit 3.57% top-5 error on ImageNet, winning ILSVRC 2015.","It removed the practical ceiling on how deep a network could be, which drove a decade of progress in computer vision. The skip-connection pattern became the standard building block in transformers, so every LLM you run today uses the same residual-stream idea.",'Every Transformer block, and so every model you run in llama.cpp, Ollama or LM Studio, adds its result back onto its input (a "residual connection"), the trick this paper introduced. It is what lets models stack dozens of layers and still train.',"The appendices on object detection (PASCAL VOC, COCO, ImageNet detection and localization) are competition results that do not change the core idea.",B.aa0,B.afj,B.anW)
B.ab3=s(["Algorithm 1: the full Adam update rule in about 10 lines of pseudocode, including the bias-correction division","Section 2.1: how the effective step size is bounded by alpha and why that makes choosing a learning rate easier","Figure 1: side-by-side training curves on MNIST and IMDB showing Adam matching Adagrad and beating SGD with Nesterov momentum"],t.s)
B.aBH=new A.o("stochastic gradient descent","Taking tiny steps using only a random batch of data, not the whole dataset.")
B.azt=new A.o("adaptive learning rate","A step size that automatically shrinks or grows per parameter based on past gradients.")
B.aCG=new A.o("bias correction","Dividing out the under-estimate caused by starting the running average at zero.")
B.aAb=new A.o("sparse gradients","Most gradient values are zero; only a few dimensions get a signal each step.")
B.aCS=new A.o("regret bound","A worst-case guarantee on how far your total loss is from the best possible.")
B.ajX=s([B.aBH,B.azt,B.aCG,B.aAb,B.aCS],t.L)
B.adr=s(["It uses the same learning rate for every parameter","It computes a separate, adaptive step size for each parameter based on gradient history","It replaces gradients with full second-order curvature matrices","It averages the last 1000 parameter vectors before each update"],t.s)
B.awP=new A.a7("What does Adam do differently from plain SGD?",B.adr,1,"Adam divides each parameter's gradient by a running estimate of gradient magnitude, giving every parameter its own effective step size.")
B.a7L=s(["To prevent the model from overfitting on the first training batch","To make the learning rate decay linearly over the course of training","To cancel out the under-estimate caused by initializing the running averages at zero","To ensure all gradient values remain positive"],t.s)
B.axD=new A.a7("Why does Adam include a bias-correction step?",B.a7L,2,"Starting the moving averages at zero makes early estimates too small, so Adam divides by (1 - beta^t) to correct that initial bias.")
B.adJ=s(["SGD and Nesterov momentum","AdaDelta and vSGD","SFO and natural gradient descent","AdaGrad and RMSProp"],t.s)
B.aws=new A.a7("Which two earlier methods did Adam combine?",B.adJ,3,"The paper states Adam was designed to combine AdaGrad's strength with sparse gradients and RMSProp's strength in non-stationary settings.")
B.anX=s([B.awP,B.axD,B.aws],t.b)
B.auD=new A.cS("SRC-ADAM","Adam","2014","Diederik P. Kingma (University of Amsterdam, OpenAI) and Jimmy Lei Ba (University of Toronto)","Adam gives every parameter its own learning rate, automatically adjusted based on how large and how consistent that parameter's gradients have been over time.","Training a neural network means nudging millions of numbers, one tiny step at a time, based on how wrong the model's last guess was. Standard methods apply the same step size to every number, which is wasteful. Adam keeps a running average of each parameter's gradient direction and a running average of how big those gradients have been, then scales each update by dividing direction by size. Parameters with consistently large gradients get smaller steps; parameters with small or noisy gradients get relatively larger ones. The paper shows Adam matches Adagrad on sparse IMDB data and beats SGD with momentum on MNIST, while a competing quasi-Newton method (SFO) runs 5-10x slower per iteration. The default settings (alpha = 0.001, beta1 = 0.9, beta2 = 0.999) worked across all tested models without tuning.","Before Adam, picking a good learning rate was a fragile, model-specific gamble that often required hours of trial and error. Adam made a single default setting work across a wide range of architectures and datasets, removing much of that guesswork. It became the go-to optimizer in virtually every deep-learning framework.","Almost every model you download was trained with Adam or its close cousin AdamW, and fine-tuning libraries such as Hugging Face Transformers, TRL and Unsloth use AdamW by default. Programs that only run models (Ollama, LM Studio, llama.cpp) never touch it: optimizers matter only while weights are being trained.","The convergence proof in Section 4 and the full Appendix (Section 10) are heavy online-convex-optimization math that a hobbyist can safely skip.",B.ab3,B.ajX,B.anX)
B.alP=s([B.aum,B.auL,B.auq,B.auD],t.v5)
B.aug=new A.l5("Before the Transformer",B.alP)
B.aaG=s(["Figure 1: The full architecture diagram showing the encoder stack on the left and the decoder stack on the right, with the three types of attention connections between them.","Section 3.2.2: Multi-Head Attention \u2014 how 8 heads run in parallel, each with its own projection, and why a single head is not enough.","Table 2: BLEU scores and training costs (FLOPs) for the Transformer versus ByteNet, GNMT, ConvS2S, and MoE, showing the quality and cost advantage."],t.s)
B.azE=new A.o("Self-attention","A word looks at every other word to decide what matters.")
B.aAw=new A.o("Multi-head attention","Several attention systems run in parallel, each focusing on different patterns.")
B.aAE=new A.o("Encoder","The part that reads and understands the input sentence.")
B.azD=new A.o("Decoder","The part that generates the output one word at a time.")
B.aAy=new A.o("Positional encoding","Numbers added to each word so the model knows the order.")
B.aor=s([B.azE,B.aAw,B.aAE,B.azD,B.aAy],t.L)
B.aos=s(["Self-attention","Larger hidden states","Convolutional filters","Memory networks"],t.s)
B.awO=new A.a7("What replaces recurrence and convolutions in the Transformer?",B.aos,0,"The paper's core contribution is using attention mechanisms alone, with no recurrence or convolutions.")
B.anL=s(["It uses a smaller vocabulary","It has fewer total parameters","It computes all positions in parallel rather than sequentially","It skips the decoder entirely"],t.s)
B.awQ=new A.a7("Why could the Transformer be trained faster than RNN-based models?",B.anL,2,"Self-attention connects all positions with a constant number of operations, enabling full parallelization.")
B.adR=s(["Reduce training cost by half","Attend to different types of relationships at different positions simultaneously","Eliminate the need for positional encoding","Process infinitely long sequences"],t.s)
B.ayq=new A.a7("What does multi-head attention allow the model to do?",B.adR,1,"Each head learns to focus on a different aspect, such as syntax or semantics, in parallel.")
B.ab2=s([B.awO,B.awQ,B.ayq],t.b)
B.auv=new A.cS("SRC-TRANSFORMER","Transformer","2017","Ashish Vaswani et al., Google Brain / Google Research / University of Toronto","Instead of reading a sentence word-by-word, the model looks at every word at once and decides which words matter for which other words. This single mechanism replaces all the step-by-step processing that older models needed.","Before this, language models processed words strictly one after another, which made training slow and hard to parallelize. The authors built a model that uses only self-attention: every word looks directly at every other word to figure out what it should focus on. The architecture has 6 layers and 8 parallel attention heads, with no recurrence or convolutions at all. On machine translation, the big model scored 28.4 BLEU on English-to-German and 41.8 BLEU on English-to-French, beating all previous single models and ensembles. Training took 3.5 days on 8 GPUs, a small fraction of the cost of competing systems.","It removed the sequential bottleneck that had made training slow and limited how far back a model could look. Because every position is computed in parallel, it scaled to much larger models and longer contexts. It became the core architecture behind virtually every modern large language model, from GPT to Llama.",'Every model you run in Ollama, LM Studio, llama.cpp or vLLM is a Transformer (most are "decoder-only" versions of it). The attention layers this paper introduced are also why longer chats need more memory: each token\'s keys and values are kept in the KV cache.',"The constituency parsing experiments (Section 6.3) and the detailed optimizer formulas (Section 5.3) can be safely skipped.",B.aaG,B.aor,B.ab2)
B.abA=s(["Figure 1: The side-by-side diagram showing how the same architecture is used for pre-training (masked LM + NSP) and fine-tuning (QA, NLI, NER).","Section 3.1: The two pre-training tasks explained in plain terms, including the 80/10/10 masking trick that avoids the pre-train/fine-tune mismatch.","Table 1: The GLUE results where BERT beats every prior system on all nine tasks, with the 80.5% average score."],t.s)
B.aAr=new A.o("masked language model","Hiding random words and asking the model to guess them from context.")
B.aD9=new A.o("self-attention","Each word looks at every other word to build its meaning.")
B.azi=new A.o("fine-tuning","Taking a pre-trained model and training it a bit more on a specific task.")
B.azr=new A.o("bidirectional","Reading text from both left and right, not just one direction.")
B.aBR=new A.o("WordPiece","A way to split words into smaller pieces the model can recognize.")
B.alE=s([B.aAr,B.aD9,B.azi,B.azr,B.aBR],t.L)
B.a84=s(["Reading text left-to-right and predicting the next word","Randomly hiding words and predicting them using context from both directions","Translating sentences between languages","Summarizing long documents"],t.s)
B.awe=new A.a7("What is BERT's main pre-training task?",B.a84,1,"BERT masks 15% of tokens at random and predicts them using context from both sides, unlike GPT which only reads left-to-right.")
B.adc=s(["Left-to-right models are too slow to train on large data","Bidirectional models use fewer parameters overall","Left-to-right models can't use right-side context, which hurts tasks like question answering","Bidirectional training requires less labeled data"],t.s)
B.awE=new A.a7("Why did the authors choose bidirectional pre-training over left-to-right?",B.adc,2,"The paper argues that for token-level tasks like QA, it is crucial to incorporate context from both directions, which a left-to-right model cannot do.")
B.ani=s(["It replaces masked words with [MASK] 80% of the time, a random word 10%, and keeps the original 10%","It simply removes all [MASK] tokens before fine-tuning","It retrains the entire model without any masking","It adds a [MASK] token to every fine-tuning input"],t.s)
B.axG=new A.a7("How does BERT handle the mismatch between pre-training (where [MASK] appears) and fine-tuning (where it does not)?",B.ani,0,"The 80/10/10 replacement strategy forces the model to maintain a contextual representation of every token, not just the masked ones.")
B.a9V=s([B.awe,B.awE,B.axG],t.b)
B.auw=new A.cS("SRC-BERT","BERT","2018","Jacob Devlin et al., Google AI Language","Instead of reading text only left-to-right, BERT hides random words and forces the model to guess them using context from both directions, giving it a two-way understanding of language before any specific task.","Before BERT, models like GPT could only read text left-to-right, which hurt performance on tasks like question answering where you need context from both sides. The authors trained a Transformer encoder by randomly masking 15% of words and forcing the model to predict them using context from both directions. They also added a second pre-training task where the model predicts whether two sentences actually follow each other. After pre-training, BERT is fine-tuned for a specific task by adding just one extra output layer and training on labeled data. The result was state-of-the-art performance on 11 NLP tasks, including a GLUE score of 80.5% and SQuAD v1.1 F1 of 93.2.","BERT proved that bidirectional pre-training is fundamentally better than left-to-right for understanding tasks, not just generation. It became the template for the pre-train-then-fine-tune recipe that shaped the next several years of NLP research.","BERT-style encoders are still everywhere, just out of sight: most embedding models used for document search (RAG), for example the small all-MiniLM and BGE models that Open WebUI can use for its knowledge bases, are BERT-family encoders. They turn text into vectors instead of writing replies.","The ablation studies in Section 5 and the implementation appendix are dense; a hobbyist can skip them and focus on the core idea in Sections 3 and 4.",B.abA,B.alE,B.a9V)
B.a8Y=s(["Figure 1: Zero-shot task performance across NLP tasks as model size grows \u2014 the core scaling story in one chart.","Section 3.5: How GPT-2 hits 55 F1 on CoQA reading comprehension without any supervised training data.","Table 2: The four model sizes (117M to 1542M parameters) and their layer counts and dimensions."],t.s)
B.aBf=new A.o("zero-shot","Doing a task with no task-specific training at all")
B.aAe=new A.o("Transformer","The architecture that lets the model weigh every word against every other")
B.aBA=new A.o("perplexity","A score where lower means the model was less surprised by the text")
B.aD5=new A.o("BLEU","A standard score for how close a machine translation is to a human one")
B.aAW=new A.o("F1","A single number combining precision and recall for answer accuracy")
B.aiT=s([B.aBf,B.aAe,B.aBA,B.aD5,B.aAW],t.L)
B.a8i=s(["It beat every supervised baseline by a wide margin","It matched or exceeded 3 of 4 baselines without using any of their 127,000+ training pairs","It was the first model to ever answer reading-comprehension questions","It used a completely different architecture than the baselines"],t.s)
B.aw8=new A.a7("What made GPT-2's results on CoQA notable?",B.a8i,1,"GPT-2 reached 55 F1 zero-shot, matching or exceeding 3 of 4 supervised baselines without any of their labeled training data.")
B.aiq=s(["Model capacity (size)","The specific web pages chosen for training","The order in which documents are presented","The tokenizer used for the input text"],t.s)
B.axF=new A.a7("According to the paper, what is the key factor that improves zero-shot task performance?",B.aiq,0,"The paper states that model capacity is essential and that increasing it improves performance in a log-linear fashion across tasks.")
B.akb=s(["A collection of Wikipedia articles and news headlines","A curated set of academic papers from arXiv","A dialogue dataset collected from customer-service transcripts","40 GB of web text scraped from 45 million Reddit-vetted links"],t.s)
B.awF=new A.a7("What was WebText, the training corpus for GPT-2?",B.akb,3,"WebText was built by scraping outbound links from Reddit posts with at least 3 karma, yielding 8 million documents totaling 40 GB.")
B.a7D=s([B.aw8,B.axF,B.awF],t.b)
B.auH=new A.cS("SRC-GPT2","GPT-2","2019","Alec Radford et al., OpenAI","A large language model trained only to predict the next word on diverse web text starts to perform tasks like answering questions and translating without ever being explicitly taught those tasks.","Before GPT-2, every NLP task needed its own labeled dataset and a separate training run. OpenAI built a 1.5B-parameter Transformer and trained it on WebText, 40 GB of web text drawn from 8 million Reddit-vetted documents. They then tested it zero-shot on eight language-modeling benchmarks and a range of downstream tasks. It hit state-of-the-art on 7 of 8 benchmarks and scored 55 F1 on CoQA reading comprehension without using any of the 127,000+ training pairs the supervised baselines relied on. The paper shows that model capacity is the key lever: bigger models transfer to more tasks in a log-linear fashion.","It proved that scale plus diverse text could replace task-specific supervision for a wide range of NLP tasks. This 'pre-train on a big corpus, then just prompt the model' recipe became the template for every chatbot and LLM that followed.","Every model you run in Ollama, LM Studio or llama.cpp follows GPT-2's recipe: pre-train on a huge pile of text to predict the next token, then just ask. Its byte-level BPE tokenizer idea also lives on in the tokenizers of many modern models.","The data-overlap analysis in Section 4 is thorough but you can skim it; the headline is that overlap is small and does not explain the results.",B.a8Y,B.aiT,B.a7D)
B.ab8=s(["Figure 1.1: The diagram that explains in-context learning as a two-loop process: broad skills from pre-training, then rapid task adaptation at inference time.","Figure 1.3: The aggregate bar chart across 42 benchmarks showing that few-shot performance rises much faster with model size than zero-shot does.","Table 3.3: The closed-book QA results where GPT-3's few-shot TriviaQA score of 71.2% beats the fine-tuned open-domain SOTA of 68.0%."],t.s)
B.az7=new A.o("few-shot learning","Giving a model a handful of examples in the prompt so it figures out the task.")
B.aBW=new A.o("in-context learning","The model adapts to a task using only the text in its input, no weight changes.")
B.aA_=new A.o("autoregressive","The model predicts the next word one at a time, building the output sequentially.")
B.azA=new A.o("context window","The maximum number of tokens the model can see at once; 2048 for GPT-3.")
B.aDr=new A.o("BLEU","A score measuring how closely machine-generated text matches human reference text.")
B.anK=s([B.az7,B.aBW,B.aA_,B.azA,B.aDr],t.L)
B.a82=s(["The model is fine-tuned on a small dataset","The model is given a few examples in the prompt with no weight updates","The model is trained on only a few sentences","The model runs on a few GPUs"],t.s)
B.ay8=new A.a7("What does 'few-shot' mean in this paper?",B.a82,1,"Few-shot means conditioning on a small number of demonstrations in the input text while performing only forward passes, with zero gradient updates.")
B.aa2=s(["175 billion","1.5 billion","17 billion","17.5 billion"],t.s)
B.axv=new A.a7("How many parameters does GPT-3 have?",B.aa2,0,"The abstract states GPT-3 is an autoregressive language model with 175 billion parameters, 10x more than any previous non-sparse model.")
B.aa7=s(["It wrote articles that were factually more accurate than human ones","It generated articles that human evaluators had difficulty distinguishing from real ones","It could summarize news in fewer words than humans","It translated news articles into six languages"],t.s)
B.ayI=new A.a7("What did GPT-3 do with news articles that the authors found notable?",B.aa7,1,"The abstract and Section 3.9 report that human evaluators had difficulty distinguishing GPT-3's synthetic news articles from articles written by humans.")
B.acZ=s([B.ay8,B.axv,B.ayI],t.b)
B.auK=new A.cS("SRC-GPT3","GPT-3","2020","Tom B. Brown et al., OpenAI","If you make a language model big enough, it can figure out a new task from just a few examples shown in the prompt text, without any weight updates or fine-tuning.","Before this, every new NLP task needed thousands of labeled examples and a round of fine-tuning. The OpenAI team trained GPT-3, a 175-billion-parameter autoregressive model on 300 billion tokens of filtered web text, and tested it on over two dozen tasks using only in-context examples. In the few-shot setting (10 to 100 examples in the prompt, zero gradient updates), GPT-3 hit 85.0 F1 on CoQA and 71.2% on TriviaQA, matching or beating fine-tuned state-of-the-art models. It also generated news articles that human evaluators had difficulty distinguishing from real ones. The team trained 8 model sizes from 125M to 175B parameters and showed that in-context learning ability grows smoothly with scale.","It proved that raw scale could unlock a new mode of use: you no longer need to retrain a model for every task, you just describe the task in text. This became the default way people interact with large language models and set the template for every chatbot and prompt-based tool that followed.","When you paste a few examples into a chat and the model copies the pattern, that is the few-shot, in-context learning this paper measured. It works with any model you run in Ollama, LM Studio or llama.cpp; bigger models are usually better at it, just as the paper found.","Section 4 (data contamination analysis) and Section 6 (broader societal impacts, bias, and energy usage) are long policy discussions that a hobbyist can safely skim.",B.ab8,B.anK,B.acZ)
B.akg=s([B.auv,B.auw,B.auH,B.auK],t.v5)
B.aud=new A.l5("The Transformer era",B.akg)
B.aoe=s(["Figure 1: The three power-law curves for model size, dataset size, and compute, all on one chart","Figure 3: How to split a 1000x increase in compute between model size, batch size, and training steps","Table 5: All the fitted power-law exponents (alpha_N, alpha_D, alpha_minC, etc.) in one compact table"],t.s)
B.azQ=new A.o("Power law","A relationship where doubling one thing gives a fixed, predictable fraction of improvement")
B.aBx=new A.o("Cross-entropy loss","A score for how surprised the model is by the next word; lower means better")
B.aCO=new A.o("Overfitting","The model memorises training text instead of learning general patterns, so it fails on new text")
B.aCk=new A.o("Compute budget","The total number of arithmetic operations you are willing to spend on training")
B.aAc=new A.o("Critical batch size","The batch size where adding more parallelism stops giving you a meaningful speed-up")
B.agj=s([B.azQ,B.aBx,B.aCO,B.aCk,B.aAc],t.L)
B.amm=s(["It drops to about 0.95x (a 5% improvement)","It drops by about 50%","It stays roughly the same","It increases slightly because the model is harder to train"],t.s)
B.awN=new A.a7("When you double the number of parameters in a language model, what happens to the loss?",B.amm,0,"The paper fits the loss as N^(-0.076), so doubling N multiplies the loss by 2^(-0.076), which is approximately 0.95.")
B.an7=s(["Train the same model for 10x longer","Use 10x more training data","Make the model roughly 5x bigger, with only a small increase in data and training steps","Split the extra compute evenly between model size, data, and training time"],t.s)
B.awv=new A.a7("You have 10x more compute than before. According to the paper, what is the optimal way to use it?",B.an7,2,"The optimal model size grows as C^0.73, so 10x compute yields about 5x the model size, while data and steps grow only modestly.")
B.aaf=s(["About 8x more data","About 5x more data","About 64x more data","No additional data is needed since the model is just bigger"],t.s)
B.awm=new A.a7("If you make your model 8 times bigger, how much more training data do you need to avoid overfitting?",B.aaf,1,"The paper shows data should scale as N^0.74, so 8x the parameters requires roughly 5x the data to stay on the compute-efficient frontier.")
B.acp=s([B.awN,B.awv,B.awm],t.b)
B.auo=new A.cS("SRC-KAPLAN","Scaling Laws","2020","Jared Kaplan et al., OpenAI","Language model quality follows predictable power-law curves as you scale up model size, data, and compute. You can use simple equations to figure out the best way to spend your training budget.","Before this paper, nobody had a clean formula for how much better a language model gets when you make it bigger, give it more data, or train it longer. Kaplan and colleagues trained hundreds of Transformer models ranging from 768 to 1.5 billion parameters on up to 22 billion tokens, and found that the loss drops as a smooth power law in each of those three factors, spanning more than seven orders of magnitude. The key practical result: if you double the parameters, the loss drops to about 0.95x (a 5% improvement), and if you make the model 8x bigger you only need about 5x more data to avoid overfitting. They also showed that when you have more compute, you should spend most of it on a bigger model (N grows as C^0.73) rather than training longer or using more data. Model shape (depth vs. width, number of heads) barely matters once you fix the total parameter count.","It gave the field a mathematical compass for building bigger models. Instead of guessing whether a 10x bigger model would be worth it, you could calculate the expected loss and the data you would need. It also showed that bigger models are more sample-efficient, which changed how people think about the data-to-model-size ratio.",'Model families come in sizes (for example 8B, 70B, 405B) because of the smooth "bigger is better" curves this paper measured: labs can predict how much better the next size will be before training it. When you choose a bigger model in Ollama or LM Studio and it is noticeably smarter, you are seeing these curves.',"The LSTM and Universal Transformer comparisons in Section 3.2.1 and the detailed derivation of the critical batch size mechanics in Section 5.1 are interesting but not essential for understanding the core scaling results.",B.aoe,B.agj,B.acp)
B.ajD=s(["Figure 1: The overlaid predictions from all three approaches plus Kaplan et al., showing that current models should be much smaller and trained much longer.","Table 2: The exponents a and b for each approach, all hovering around 0.5 for both parameters and tokens, versus Kaplan's 0.73 and 0.27.","Section 4.2: The head-to-head results where 70B Chinchilla beats 280B Gopher, 175B GPT-3, and 530B MT-NLG across MMLU, BIG-bench, reading comprehension, and QA."],t.s)
B.aCq=new A.o("FLOPs","Total floating-point operations a model performs during training.")
B.aCQ=new A.o("Compute-optimal","The best balance of model size and data for a fixed budget.")
B.azg=new A.o("Scaling law","A predictable formula linking model size, data, and performance.")
B.aAo=new A.o("MMLU","A 57-subject multiple-choice test of general knowledge.")
B.aD6=new A.o("Under-trained","A model that has seen too little data for its size.")
B.akK=s([B.aCq,B.aCQ,B.azg,B.aAo,B.aD6],t.L)
B.aaM=s(["Tokens should stay the same","Tokens should increase by a factor of 1.8","Tokens should also double","Tokens should quadruple"],t.s)
B.ayb=new A.a7("According to the paper, what should happen to training tokens when you double the model size?",B.aaM,2,"All three approaches found exponents near 0.5 for both parameters and tokens, meaning they scale equally.")
B.ajH=s(["It was trained on 4 times more tokens (1.4T vs 300B)","It used a different architecture with more layers","It was trained on a different language","It used a larger batch size"],t.s)
B.avN=new A.a7("Why could Chinchilla (70B) outperform Gopher (280B) on the same compute budget?",B.ajH,0,"Chinchilla used the same FLOP budget but allocated it to a smaller model trained on 1.4 trillion tokens instead of 300 billion.")
B.aoz=s(["Models were too small to be useful","Training data was too noisy","Learning rates were set too high","Model size grew while training data stayed roughly constant"],t.s)
B.ayx=new A.a7("What was the core problem with the pre-Chinchilla approach to scaling LLMs?",B.aoz,3,"Most models from that era were trained on around 300B tokens regardless of size, leaving larger models under-trained.")
B.aim=s([B.ayb,B.avN,B.ayx],t.b)
B.auk=new A.cS("SRC-CHINCHILLA","Chinchilla","2022","Jordan Hoffmann et al., DeepMind","For a fixed compute budget, you should grow model size and training data at the same rate, not just make the model bigger and feed it the same amount of text.","The field kept building bigger models but training them on roughly the same ~300 billion tokens, assuming more parameters meant better performance. Hoffmann and colleagues trained over 400 models (from 70M to 16B parameters, on 5B to 500B tokens) and used three separate methods to find the best size-and-data split for a given FLOP budget. All three methods agreed: scale parameters and tokens equally. They then built Chinchilla, a 70B-parameter model trained on 1.4 trillion tokens, using the same compute as Gopher (280B parameters, 300B tokens). Chinchilla beat Gopher, GPT-3, Jurassic-1, and MT-NLG 530B on nearly every benchmark, scoring 67.5% on MMLU, a 7-point gain over Gopher. The smaller size also meant far lower inference and fine-tuning costs.","It showed that the 'bigger is better' race was wasting compute: a smaller model trained much longer outperforms a larger model trained briefly. This shifted the industry toward training on trillions of tokens with modest parameter counts, making strong models cheaper to run on everyday hardware.",'Every size choice in an open model family is shaped by this paper. Later models went even further than Chinchilla\'s "about 20 tokens per parameter": small models such as Llama 3 8B were trained on many trillions of tokens because a small, well-fed model is cheap to run on home hardware. That is why a 7-9B model in Ollama or LM Studio is far better than older models of the same size.',"The three different fitting methodologies in Section 3 (Huber loss, L-BFGS, parametric loss decomposition) are the mathematical plumbing; the conclusion is the same regardless of which method you follow.",B.ajD,B.akK,B.aim)
B.amc=s([B.auo,B.auk],t.v5)
B.auj=new A.l5("Scaling",B.amc)
B.abs=s(["Figure 2: the three-step diagram showing SFT, reward model training, and PPO in sequence","Section 4.1: the main human-preference results where 1.3B InstructGPT beats 175B GPT-3","Section 4.2: truthfulness and toxicity results on public benchmarks, including the 21% vs 41% hallucination numbers"],t.s)
B.aCx=new A.o("RLHF","Training a model to maximize a reward based on human preferences")
B.aDb=new A.o("Reward model","A small model that scores responses, trained on human rankings")
B.aDC=new A.o("PPO","The reinforcement-learning algorithm used to fine-tune against the reward")
B.azu=new A.o("Alignment tax","Performance drop on other tasks when you fine-tune for human preferences")
B.aAO=new A.o("Hallucination","The model making up facts not present in the input")
B.amV=s([B.aCx,B.aDb,B.aDC,B.azu,B.aAO],t.L)
B.aeX=s(["Supervised fine-tuning on demonstrations, then reinforcement learning against a reward model trained on human rankings","Pretraining from scratch, then a second pretraining pass on instruction data","Filtering the training corpus, then distilling into a smaller model","Training a classifier, then using it to reject bad outputs"],t.s)
B.axd=new A.a7("What is the core two-step training process described in this paper?",B.aeX,0,"The paper explicitly lays out SFT (Step 1) followed by PPO against a reward model trained on human comparisons (Steps 2 and 3).")
B.ahY=s(["It used a different architecture that is more efficient","It was fine-tuned on human preference data while GPT-3 was not","It was trained on a larger internet corpus","It used a more advanced tokenizer"],t.s)
B.ayh=new A.a7("Why could the 1.3B InstructGPT model outperform the 175B GPT-3 in human evaluations?",B.ahY,1,"Both models share the GPT-3 architecture; the only difference is that InstructGPT was fine-tuned on human demonstrations and rankings.")
B.a8C=s(["It trains the reward model on a larger dataset","It replaces the reward model with a rule-based scorer","It mixes pretraining-distribution gradients into the RL updates to reduce performance regressions on public benchmarks","It uses a higher learning rate to converge faster"],t.s)
B.ay0=new A.a7("What does the PPO-ptx variant do differently from plain PPO?",B.a8C,2,"PPO-ptx adds a pretraining loss term (gamma) to the RL objective, which mitigates the alignment tax on tasks like SQuAD and translation.")
B.a9C=s([B.axd,B.ayh,B.ay0],t.b)
B.aux=new A.cS("SRC-INSTRUCTGPT","InstructGPT","2022","Long Ouyang et al., OpenAI","Instead of making a language model bigger, you can make it follow instructions better by training it on human preferences: first show it good examples, then reward it for outputs that humans rank above others.","Large language models make up facts, generate toxic text, and often ignore what the user actually asked for. The OpenAI team fine-tuned GPT-3 in two steps: first supervised learning on human-written demonstrations, then reinforcement learning using a reward model trained on human rankings of model outputs. The 1.3B InstructGPT model was preferred over the 175B GPT-3 in head-to-head human evaluations, despite having 100x fewer parameters. The 175B InstructGPT won 85% of the time against 175B GPT-3. On closed-domain tasks it hallucinates at 21% versus 41% for GPT-3, and it generates about 25% fewer toxic outputs when prompted to be respectful.","It proved that a smaller, aligned model beats a larger unaligned one, and that human preference data is a practical way to steer model behavior. This two-step recipe (supervised fine-tuning, then RLHF) became the standard method behind ChatGPT and most modern instruction-tuned models.",'Almost every chat model you use, open or closed, went through a version of this recipe: supervised fine-tuning on good examples, then preference tuning (RLHF as in this paper, or later variants such as DPO). The "Instruct" or "Chat" in a model\'s name on Hugging Face means it went through this step; the "Base" version did not.',"Section 2 (related work) and the extended discussion in Section 5 are dense with citations and can be safely skimmed.",B.abs,B.amV,B.a9C)
B.aeV=s(["Figure 1: The two-stage pipeline (supervised then RL) shown at a glance, with the critique-revision loop on top and the AI-feedback RL loop on the bottom.","Section 3.1: The worked example where the model critiques and revises its own advice about hacking a neighbor's wifi, showing exactly how a principle steers the revision.","Figure 4: Accuracy on 438 binary comparison questions, showing that chain-of-thought reasoning pushes larger models toward the performance of human-feedback-trained preference models."],t.s)
B.aAl=new A.o("Constitution","A short list of written rules that guide how the AI should behave.")
B.aA7=new A.o("RLAIF","Reinforcement learning from AI feedback; the AI judges its own outputs instead of humans.")
B.aB0=new A.o("Chain-of-thought","Asking the model to write out its reasoning step by step before answering.")
B.aDz=new A.o("Evasiveness","When an AI dodges a question instead of engaging with it.")
B.aBX=new A.o("Preference model","A small model that scores which of two responses is better.")
B.aoE=s([B.aAl,B.aA7,B.aB0,B.aDz,B.aBX],t.L)
B.ahQ=s(["A larger dataset of human labels","A list of 16 written principles","A rule-based if-else program","A separate neural network trained from scratch"],t.s)
B.awW=new A.a7("What replaces human labels for harmlessness in Constitutional AI?",B.ahQ,1,"The paper uses 16 human-written principles as the sole source of harmlessness oversight, with no human labels for that dimension.")
B.ad4=s(["Critiques and revises them","Deletes them entirely","Translates them to another language","Reverses the word order"],t.s)
B.axw=new A.a7("In the supervised phase, what does the model do to its own responses?",B.ad4,0,"The model generates a critique of its response according to a principle, then revises the response in light of that critique.")
B.aeu=s(["To make the model faster","To reduce GPU memory usage","To improve accuracy and transparency of the AI's judgments","To eliminate the need for a preference model"],t.s)
B.awD=new A.a7("Why did the team use chain-of-thought reasoning in the RL phase?",B.aeu,2,"Chain-of-thought reasoning significantly improved the feedback model's accuracy on comparison questions and made the AI's decision-making more legible.")
B.aoH=s([B.awW,B.axw,B.awD],t.b)
B.auQ=new A.cS("SRC-CAI","Constitutional AI","2022","Yuntao Bai et al., Anthropic","Instead of paying humans to label every harmful AI response, you give the model a short list of written rules (a 'constitution') and let it critique, revise, and judge its own outputs against those rules.","Training AI to be harmless used to require tens of thousands of human labels, and the resulting models became evasive and unhelpful. The Anthropic team replaced human harmlessness labels with AI-generated ones, steered by 16 written principles. In a supervised phase, the model critiques and revises its own responses, then is fine-tuned on the revisions. In an RL phase, a separate model judges pairs of responses against the principles, and the assistant is trained with that AI feedback. The result: the RL-CAI model was significantly more harmless than human-feedback-trained models while staying helpful, and it was virtually never evasive.",'It showed that a short, readable list of principles plus AI feedback can replace much of the human labelling for harmlessness, and that the model\'s reasoning about those principles can be inspected in plain language. "RL from AI feedback" became a standard tool in post-training.','Anthropic uses this approach to train Claude, so the way Claude explains a refusal comes from training like this. You cannot run Constitutional AI training in a chat app, but its central idea, a model judging outputs against written principles ("AI feedback"), is now common in how labs build preference data for open models too.',"The detailed hyperparameters, batch sizes, and training infrastructure details in Section 4.2, plus the per-person contribution breakdown in Section 7.",B.aeV,B.aoE,B.aoH)
B.aiE=s(["Figure 1: Side-by-side comparison of the RLHF pipeline (reward model + RL) versus DPO (direct classification loss).","Section 4, Eq. 5 and Eq. 7: The key reparameterization that turns the reward into a function of the policy, and the resulting DPO loss.","Figure 2 (left): The reward-KL frontier showing DPO strictly dominates PPO on sentiment generation, even PPO with ground-truth rewards."],t.s)
B.aCM=new A.o("RLHF","Training a model with human feedback using reinforcement learning")
B.azz=new A.o("Reward model","A separate network that scores how good a response is")
B.aAa=new A.o("PPO","A reinforcement learning algorithm that updates the policy in small, safe steps")
B.azf=new A.o("Bradley-Terry model","A statistical model that turns reward scores into a probability of preference")
B.az3=new A.o("KL-divergence","A measure of how far the new model has drifted from the original")
B.abr=s([B.aCM,B.azz,B.aAa,B.azf,B.az3],t.L)
B.alN=s(["The reward model and the RL training loop","The need for human preference labels","The need for a reference model","The need for sampling at inference time"],t.s)
B.axX=new A.a7("What does DPO eliminate from the standard RLHF pipeline?",B.alN,0,"DPO skips both the separate reward model and the RL optimization step, replacing them with a single classification loss.")
B.an2=s(["It maximizes the absolute probability of the preferred response","It samples N responses and picks the highest-scoring one","It minimizes a binary cross-entropy loss on preference pairs","It computes the KL divergence between two reward models"],t.s)
B.ayn=new A.a7("How does DPO's training loss work?",B.an2,2,"DPO treats each preference pair as a binary classification and minimizes the negative log-likelihood of the preferred response being ranked above the dispreferred one.")
B.aoo=s(["To increase the sampling temperature automatically","To prevent model degeneration when the implicit reward misorders completions","To normalize reward scores to zero mean","To match the KL constraint exactly at every training step"],t.s)
B.avW=new A.a7("Why does DPO include a per-example importance weight in its gradient?",B.aoo,1,"The weight scales the update by how incorrectly the implicit reward ranks the pair, which the authors show prevents the model from degenerating.")
B.adX=s([B.axX,B.ayn,B.avW],t.b)
B.auP=new A.cS("SRC-DPO","DPO","2023","Rafael Rafailov et al., Stanford University and CZ Biohub","The language model you are fine-tuning already is the reward model. You can skip the separate reward model and the reinforcement learning loop entirely, and just train the model with a simple classification loss on preference pairs.","Aligning a language model with human preferences used to require a two-step RLHF pipeline: first train a reward model, then use reinforcement learning (PPO) to fine-tune the LM against that reward. This process was complex, unstable, and expensive. The authors showed that the optimal policy under the standard RLHF objective has a closed-form expression, so you can rewrite the loss as a function of the policy directly. DPO minimizes a binary cross-entropy loss on preference pairs, with no reward model, no RL loop, and no sampling from the policy during training. On summarization, DPO reached a win rate of approximately 61% against reference summaries at temperature 0.0, beating PPO's 57%. On single-turn dialogue, DPO was the only computationally efficient method that improved over the preferred completions, using models up to 6B parameters.","DPO turned preference-based alignment into a single, stable training step that looks like ordinary supervised learning. It removed the need to train a separate reward model, run an RL loop, and sample from the policy during training, which cut compute costs and hyperparameter tuning. This lowered the barrier for labs and hobbyists to align models with human preferences.",'Fine-tuning libraries such as Hugging Face TRL, Axolotl and Unsloth offer DPO as a ready-made training mode, and many open-weight chat models were preference-tuned with DPO or one of its many variants. If you ever fine-tune a model on "chosen vs rejected" answer pairs, this is the method you are using.',"The theoretical analysis in Section 5 (equivalence classes of reward functions, Plackett-Luce models, and the actor-critic instability argument) is dense and can be safely skipped on a first read.",B.aiE,B.abr,B.adX)
B.ana=s(["Figure 1: Side-by-side comparison of standard prompting (direct answer) versus chain-of-thought prompting (reasoning steps then answer) on the same math problem.","Section 3.2: The three key takeaways, including the finding that chain-of-thought is an emergent ability of model scale and that gains are larger on harder problems.","Figure 4: Scaling curves across LaMDA, GPT, and PaLM model sizes showing that chain-of-thought only helps once you hit around 100B parameters."],t.s)
B.aCX=new A.o("Chain-of-thought","Writing out intermediate reasoning steps in plain language before giving the final answer.")
B.azd=new A.o("Few-shot prompting","Giving a model a handful of example input-output pairs to show what to do.")
B.aD8=new A.o("Emergent ability","A capability that only appears once a model is large enough; small models lack it.")
B.aA8=new A.o("In-context learning","Learning a task from examples in the prompt, with no weight updates.")
B.aCg=new A.o("GSM8K","A benchmark of grade-school math word problems used to test arithmetic reasoning.")
B.acD=s([B.aCX,B.azd,B.aD8,B.aA8,B.aCg],t.L)
B.afr=s(["The model is given a calculator to verify its answer","The prompt examples include intermediate reasoning steps written before the final answer","The model is finetuned on a large reasoning dataset","The prompt is written in a formal symbolic language"],t.s)
B.ayA=new A.a7("What is the key difference between chain-of-thought prompting and standard few-shot prompting?",B.afr,1,"Chain-of-thought prompting augments each few-shot exemplar with a natural-language reasoning trace, while standard prompting only shows the input and final output.")
B.ang=s(["Any model, even 350M parameters","Only models over 1 trillion parameters","Around 100B parameters","Only models trained after 2022"],t.s)
B.awd=new A.a7("At what model scale does chain-of-thought prompting start to improve performance?",B.ang,2,"The paper shows chain-of-thought is an emergent ability: models below roughly 100B parameters produce fluent but illogical chains and actually score worse than with standard prompting.")
B.abt=s(["46% of the chains were almost correct with minor mistakes; 54% had major errors in understanding or coherence","All 50 chains were completely wrong from the first step","The chains were always logically correct but the final arithmetic was wrong","Small models produced longer, more detailed chains than large models"],t.s)
B.aw_=new A.a7("What did the authors find when they examined chains of thought for wrong answers produced by LaMDA 137B on GSM8K?",B.abt,0,"The manual analysis of 50 wrong-answer samples found 46% of chains were nearly correct (calculator error, missing step) and 54% had major semantic or coherence errors.")
B.anD=s([B.ayA,B.awd,B.aw_],t.b)
B.aup=new A.cS("SRC-COT","Chain-of-Thought","2022","Jason Wei et al., Google Research, Brain Team","If you show a large language model a few examples where it writes out its reasoning steps before giving an answer, it starts doing the same thing on new problems and gets much better at multi-step tasks.","Large language models could get bigger and bigger, but they still stumbled on tasks that require more than one step of reasoning, like solving a math word problem. The authors at Google showed that simply including a few prompt examples where the model writes out its intermediate steps in plain English before stating the answer fixes this. PaLM 540B with just 8 such exemplars scored 57% on the GSM8K math benchmark, compared to 18% with standard prompting and 55% for the prior state-of-the-art finetuned model. The effect is an emergent property of scale: models below roughly 100B parameters actually perform worse when asked to reason step by step. The technique worked across arithmetic, commonsense, and symbolic reasoning tasks. No model was finetuned; the entire method is just a different way to write the prompt.","It showed that how you ask can matter as much as model size: with a few worked examples, large models solved far more multi-step maths and logic problems. It led directly to training models to reason before answering.",'Asking a model to "think step by step" is chain-of-thought prompting. Today\'s reasoning models (the "thinking" modes in chat apps, or open models that write a <think> section before answering) are trained to do this on their own, so the idea moved from the prompt into the training.',"The ablation study in Section 3.3 and the robustness-to-annotator experiments in Section 3.4 are interesting for researchers but not essential for understanding the core idea.",B.ana,B.acD,B.anD)
B.anp=s(["Figure 1: The one-page diagram showing how the retriever (query encoder + document index) feeds passages into the BART generator, and how the two are trained jointly.","Table 1: The head-to-head scores on four open-domain QA benchmarks, where RAG-Sequence outperforms both extractive and parametric-only baselines.","Section 4.5 (Index hot-swapping): The experiment where swapping a 2016 Wikipedia index for a 2018 one changes answers about world leaders without any retraining."],t.s)
B.aAA=new A.o("parametric memory","Facts stored inside the model's own weights.")
B.aBO=new A.o("non-parametric memory","External text (like Wikipedia) the model looks up at query time.")
B.azm=new A.o("retriever","A small model that finds the most relevant passages for a question.")
B.aCl=new A.o("marginalize","Average over all candidate passages to produce one final answer.")
B.agB=s([B.aAA,B.aBO,B.azm,B.aCl],t.L)
B.aio=s(["A language model's internal knowledge plus passages retrieved from an external text index","Two language models that vote on the most likely answer","A search engine query and a keyword-matching filter","A single model that memorizes every Wikipedia article in its weights"],t.s)
B.awR=new A.a7("What does RAG combine to produce an answer?",B.aio,0,"RAG pairs a pre-trained seq2seq generator (parametric memory) with a dense-vector retriever over a Wikipedia index (non-parametric memory).")
B.aat=s(["The generator learns a new embedding space each time new text is added","The external document index can be swapped out, and the generator simply reads from whatever index is present","The model re-runs backpropagation over the new documents at inference time","The retriever is fine-tuned on the new data while the generator stays frozen"],t.s)
B.avX=new A.a7("Why can RAG's knowledge be updated without retraining the generator?",B.aat,1,"The paper demonstrates that replacing the 2016 Wikipedia index with the 2018 index immediately changes answers, with no retraining of the BART generator.")
B.adD=s(["RAG was more factual in 42.7% of cases; BART was more factual in 7.1%","Both models were judged equally factual in most cases","BART was more factual in 42.7% of cases; RAG in 7.1%","RAG was more factual in 71% of cases; BART in 90%"],t.s)
B.ayp=new A.a7("In the Jeopardy question-generation test, how did human evaluators rate RAG-Token versus BART on factuality?",B.adD,0,"Table 4 and Section 4.3 report that evaluators picked RAG as more factual in 42.7% of 452 pairs, versus 7.1% for BART.")
B.abS=s([B.awR,B.avX,B.ayp],t.b)
B.auJ=new A.cS("SRC-RAG","RAG","2020","Patrick Lewis et al., Facebook AI Research / University College London / New York University","Before generating an answer, the model looks up relevant passages from an external text index and uses them as extra context, so it can answer questions it does not have memorized.","Pre-trained language models store facts in their weights, but they cannot easily update that knowledge or show where an answer came from. The authors paired a BART generator (400M parameters) with a dense-vector retriever over 21 million Wikipedia passages and fine-tuned the two together end-to-end. On open-domain question answering, RAG-Sequence hit 44.5 exact match on Natural Questions, beating the 34.5 from a T5-11B model that relies only on its parameters. On Jeopardy question generation, human evaluators judged RAG more factual in 42.7% of 452 head-to-head comparisons versus 7.1% for BART. The team also showed that swapping the Wikipedia index from a 2016 dump to a 2018 dump updated the model's world knowledge without any retraining.","It showed that a model can look things up instead of memorising everything: the index can be updated without retraining, and the passages it used can be shown as sources. RAG became one of the most common ways to build AI assistants on private data.","\"Chat with your documents\" features work this way: Open WebUI's knowledge bases and LM Studio's document chat split your files into chunks, find the closest ones to your question with an embedding model and paste them into the prompt. Web search in chat apps is the same idea with the internet as the index.","The formal probability equations in Section 2.1 (the RAG-Sequence and RAG-Token marginalization formulas) are dense notation that a hobbyist can safely skip without losing the big picture.",B.anp,B.agB,B.abS)
B.afT=s(["Figure 1: Side-by-side comparison of Standard, CoT, Act-only, and ReAct trajectories on a HotpotQA question and an ALFWorld game, showing exactly how thoughts and actions interleave.","Table 1: PaLM-540B prompting results on HotpotQA and Fever, where you can see ReAct beat CoT on Fever (60.9 vs 56.3) and the combined ReAct+CoT-SC methods scored highest overall.","Table 2: Breakdown of success and failure modes for ReAct and CoT, showing that CoT hallucinates in 56% of its failures while ReAct's main failure is reasoning error at 47%."],t.s)
B.aA9=new A.o("chain-of-thought prompting","Asking the model to write its reasoning steps before giving the final answer.")
B.aBQ=new A.o("hallucination","The model states something as fact that is not actually true.")
B.aCv=new A.o("few-shot prompting","Giving the model a handful of worked examples to copy the pattern from.")
B.aC8=new A.o("action space","The set of things the model is allowed to do, like search or look up.")
B.aAZ=new A.o("interleaved","Alternating between two things step by step rather than doing one then the other.")
B.ab4=s([B.aA9,B.aBQ,B.aCv,B.aC8,B.aAZ],t.L)
B.al5=s(["It lets the model search external sources between reasoning steps","It trains the model from scratch on new data","It removes the need for any reasoning","It uses reinforcement learning to pick actions"],t.s)
B.avO=new A.a7("What does ReAct do that pure chain-of-thought (CoT) does not?",B.al5,0,"ReAct interleaves reasoning with actions that retrieve information from an external source like Wikipedia, grounding each step in real data.")
B.a8Z=s(["The model is trained on more data","The model grounds its reasoning in retrieved external information","The model uses a larger context window","The model avoids all reasoning steps"],t.s)
B.ayw=new A.a7("Why does ReAct produce fewer hallucinated facts than CoT?",B.a8Z,1,"Because ReAct can search and read actual Wikipedia passages, it checks its claims against real text instead of relying on internal memory.")
B.aj4=s(["30","6","1 or 2","10"],t.s)
B.ayB=new A.a7("In the ALFWorld and WebShop experiments, how many in-context examples did ReAct use to outperform methods trained on 10^3 to 10^5 task instances?",B.aj4,2,"The paper reports that one or two in-context examples were enough for ReAct to beat agents trained on thousands of episodes.")
B.aiw=s([B.avO,B.ayw,B.ayB],t.b)
B.auN=new A.cS("SRC-REACT","ReAct","2022","Shunyu Yao et al., Princeton University and Google Research","Let a language model alternate between thinking out loud and taking a real action (like searching a wiki page), so each step feeds the next and the model can check its own facts against the outside world.","Large language models could reason step by step (chain-of-thought) or generate actions in an environment, but each approach alone had blind spots: pure reasoning hallucinated facts, and pure acting lost track of the plan. The authors prompted a frozen 540B-parameter model to write a short reasoning thought, then take an action such as searching Wikipedia, then read the result, then think again, using only one to six human-written examples. On the ALFWorld text game, this one-to-two-example prompt beat imitation and reinforcement learning agents trained on thousands of episodes by 34 percentage points in success rate. On WebShop, the same approach improved success rate by 10 points over the best prior method. On fact verification, ReAct scored 60.9% versus 56.3% for chain-of-thought, because the model could verify claims against Wikipedia instead of relying on memory alone.","It showed that you do not need to train a model to use tools; a simple prompt that interleaves thinking and acting already produces grounded, interpretable behavior. It became the template for how modern LLM agents work: think, call a tool, read the result, think again. The reasoning traces make it easy for a human to see why the model did what it did and to correct it mid-task.","Agents work in a think \u2192 act \u2192 observe loop like this: when an assistant searches the web, runs code or calls a tool (for example through MCP in Claude Code or Open WebUI), it decides an action, reads the result, and thinks again. Agent frameworks such as LangChain named their basic agent after this paper.","The related work section and the ethics statement, which survey prior methods and discuss safety considerations that do not change how you would use the idea in practice.",B.afT,B.ab4,B.aiw)
B.ab7=s(["Figure 2: The three-step pipeline (sample API calls, execute them, filter by loss reduction) that defines the entire method.","Table 4: The math benchmarks where Toolformer (40.4 on ASDiv) clearly outperforms GPT-3 (14.0) and OPT (6.0).","Figure 4: Shows that tool-use ability only emerges around 775M parameters; smaller GPT-2 models do not benefit from tools."],t.s)
