B.auy=new A.cS("SRC-TOOLFORMER","Toolformer","2023","Timo Schick et al., Meta AI Research / Universitat Pompeu Fabra","A language model can teach itself to use external tools (a calculator, a search engine, a translator) without any human-labeled data, by keeping only the tool calls that actually help it predict the next words.","Large language models are strong at language but weak at arithmetic, factual lookup, and knowing the current date. The authors let a 6.7B-parameter GPT-J model propose API calls to five tools on a large text corpus, then kept only the calls that reduced the model's loss on the tokens that follow. They finetuned the model on those filtered calls. The result: Toolformer beat GPT-3 (175B) on math benchmarks (40.4 vs 14.0 on ASDiv) and fact-filling tasks (53.5 vs 39.8 on T-REx), while its language modeling perplexity stayed the same. The model used the calculator for 97.9% of math examples and the Q&A tool for 98.1% of fact examples, deciding on its own when to call each tool.","It showed that a model can learn when a tool call helps by checking whether the result makes the following text easier to predict, with only a few examples per tool. It was an early step toward the function-calling models that agents rely on.","Tool calling (\"function calling\") is now built into most models and engines: vLLM, llama.cpp's server and LM Studio expose it through their OpenAI-compatible APIs, and front-ends use it for web search, calculators or MCP tools. Today it is mostly taught with fine-tuning on tool-use examples rather than Toolformer's self-supervised trick.","The multilingual QA (MLQA) and temporal dataset sections (Tables 6 and 7) are interesting but tangential; a hobbyist can skip them and the related-work discussion without losing the core idea.",B.ab7,B.ao1,B.aaR)
B.aig=s([B.aux,B.auQ,B.auP,B.aup,B.auJ,B.auN,B.auy],t.v5)
B.aui=new A.l5("From model to assistant",B.aig)
