B.auF=new A.cS("SRC-LORA","LoRA","2021","Edward Hu et al., Microsoft","Freeze the big model and only train two tiny matrices whose product captures the small change a task needs. At inference time the model runs exactly as before, with no extra latency.","Full fine-tuning of a 175-billion-parameter model like GPT-3 means storing and updating every weight, which is prohibitively expensive in memory and storage. LoRA freezes all original weights and instead trains two small matrices (A and B) whose product approximates the change each layer requires. The authors show that even a rank as small as 1 or 2 captures enough of the needed change, even when the full dimension is 12,288. On GPT-3 175B, LoRA cuts trainable parameters by 10,000 times and GPU memory by 3 times. It matches or beats full fine-tuning on quality while adding zero extra inference latency.","Before LoRA, adapting a large model to a new task meant either retraining the entire model or adding layers that slowed down every inference call. LoRA made it practical to fine-tune big models on consumer hardware and to swap between many task-specific adapters cheaply. It became the default approach for efficient fine-tuning in the open-source LLM ecosystem.","Most fine-tunes you find on Hugging Face were trained with LoRA or QLoRA (for example with Unsloth, Axolotl or Hugging Face PEFT). llama.cpp can load a LoRA adapter on top of a base model (--lora), Ollama supports it with the ADAPTER line in a Modelfile, and vLLM can serve many LoRA adapters on one base model at the same time.","The subspace-similarity math in Section 7.2 (Grassmann distance, singular value decomposition) is interesting but not essential to understanding how LoRA works in practice.",B.anU,B.aok,B.a8b)
B.ajR=s(["Figure 1: Side-by-side memory comparison of full finetuning, LoRA, and QLoRA, showing how quantization and paged optimizers close the gap.","Section 3: The three core innovations \u2014 4-bit NormalFloat, Double Quantization, and Paged Optimizers \u2014 explained step by step.","Table 6: Vicuna benchmark scores for every model and dataset combination, showing Guanaco 65B at 99.3% of ChatGPT."],t.s)
B.aD_=new A.o("Quantization","Storing numbers with fewer bits, trading a little precision for much less memory.")
B.aAs=new A.o("LoRA (Low-Rank Adapter)","A small set of extra weights added to a frozen model so you can train it cheaply.")
B.aCI=new A.o("NormalFloat (NF4)","A 4-bit number format where each bin holds an equal share of normally distributed values.")
B.aCu=new A.o("Double Quantization","Quantizing the quantization constants themselves to save a few extra bits per parameter.")
B.aDD=new A.o("Paged Optimizer","Automatically moving optimizer data to CPU RAM when the GPU runs out of memory.")
B.aoN=s([B.aD_,B.aAs,B.aCI,B.aCu,B.aDD],t.L)
B.alf=s(["They are retrained from scratch","They are deleted to save memory","They stay frozen at 4-bit while only small adapter weights are updated","They are converted to 8-bit for faster computation"],t.s)
B.axS=new A.a7("What happens to the base model weights during QLoRA training?",B.alf,2,"QLoRA backpropagates gradients through the frozen 4-bit weights to update only the LoRA adapters.")
B.am4=s(["It is information-theoretically optimal for normally distributed data, so each quantization bin holds an equal number of values","It uses fewer total bits per parameter","It always represents zero with no error","It compresses text more efficiently"],t.s)
B.awo=new A.a7("Why does 4-bit NormalFloat (NF4) outperform regular 4-bit integers for quantizing neural network weights?",B.am4,0,"NF4 is designed for zero-centered normal distributions, which is what neural network weights follow, giving better precision than integer quantization.")
B.afw=s(["The model's output tokens","The text prompts","The LoRA adapter weights","The quantization constants from the first quantization step"],t.s)
B.axI=new A.a7("What does Double Quantization actually quantize?",B.afw,3,"Double Quantization takes the 32-bit quantization constants and compresses them to 8-bit, saving about 0.37 bits per parameter.")
B.aif=s([B.axS,B.awo,B.axI],t.b)
B.auC=new A.cS("SRC-QLORA","QLoRA","2023","Tim Dettmers et al., University of Washington","Freeze the base model at 4-bit and train only tiny adapter weights. This slashes training memory so a 65B model fits on one 48 GB GPU without losing accuracy.","Finetuning a 65B-parameter model in full 16-bit precision needs more than 780 GB of GPU memory, far beyond what a single card can hold. QLoRA keeps the base model frozen at 4-bit using a new data type called NormalFloat, and trains only small LoRA adapter weights. Three extra tricks keep memory low: Double Quantization compresses the quantization constants themselves, and Paged Optimizers offload optimizer states to CPU RAM when the GPU runs out of space. The result is a 65B model finetuned on a single 48 GB GPU in 24 hours, reaching 99.3% of ChatGPT's score on the Vicuna chatbot benchmark. The authors trained over 1,000 models this way and found that a small, high-quality dataset (9k samples) beats a much larger one (450k samples) for chatbot performance.","Before QLoRA, adapting the largest open models to your own data required multi-GPU servers. Now a single consumer or professional GPU is enough, which means hobbyists and small teams can personalize large models at home. It became the default method for fine-tuning open-weight models on a budget.","QLoRA is how most hobbyists fine-tune: libraries such as Hugging Face PEFT with bitsandbytes, Unsloth and Axolotl let you train LoRA adapters on a 4-bit base model on a single consumer GPU. The NF4 data type from this paper is the bitsandbytes 4-bit format; it is not the same thing as llama.cpp's GGUF quants.","The qualitative analysis in Section 6.1 (lemons and cherries) is fun reading but not essential to understanding the method.",B.ajR,B.aoN,B.aif)
B.ag1=s([B.auF,B.auC],t.v5)
B.auh=new A.l5("Fine-tuning on a budget",B.ag1)
B.anR=s(["Figure 1: The left panel shows the GPU memory hierarchy (SRAM vs. HBM) and how tiling avoids writing the N-by-N matrix to HBM; the right panel shows the 7.6x speedup on attention alone.","Section 3.2: The IO complexity analysis proving FlashAttention needs far fewer HBM accesses than standard attention, plus the lower-bound result.","Table 2: Side-by-side training times for GPT-2 small and medium with HuggingFace, Megatron-LM, and FlashAttention, showing up to 3x speedup."],t.s)
B.aCc=new A.o("HBM (High Bandwidth Memory)","The GPU's main fast memory; much slower than on-chip SRAM but far faster than CPU RAM.")
B.aBD=new A.o("SRAM","The tiny, extremely fast on-chip memory on a GPU chip, used for scratch computation.")
B.aCZ=new A.o("Tiling","Breaking a large matrix computation into small blocks that fit in fast memory, then processing block by block.")
B.aB5=new A.o("IO-aware","Designing an algorithm to minimize how often data is copied between fast and slow memory.")
B.aaT=s([B.aCc,B.aBD,B.aCZ,B.aB5],t.L)
B.aef=s(["It reduces the number of times data is copied between fast and slow GPU memory.","It uses fewer arithmetic operations (FLOPs) overall.","It approximates the attention matrix with a smaller one.","It runs the attention computation on the CPU instead of the GPU."],t.s)
B.awa=new A.a7("What is the main reason FlashAttention is faster than standard attention?",B.aef,0,"FlashAttention actually has more FLOPs (due to recomputation), but it cuts HBM accesses dramatically, which is the real bottleneck.")
B.akz=s(["It deletes the matrix after the forward pass finishes.","It stores the matrix in CPU RAM to save GPU memory.","It recomputes the attention values on-chip from the stored output and a few normalization statistics.","It uses a smaller low-rank approximation of the matrix."],t.s)
B.ay5=new A.a7("Why can FlashAttention skip storing the full N-by-N attention matrix for the backward pass?",B.akz,2,"By saving only the output O and the softmax statistics (m and l), FlashAttention can recompute S and P on-chip during backprop, avoiding a large HBM read.")
B.akf=s(["Compressing the attention matrix into a smaller grid of values.","Running multiple attention heads in parallel on different GPUs.","Replacing the softmax with a simpler averaging step.","Splitting the input matrices into small blocks that fit in fast on-chip memory, then computing attention block by block."],t.s)
B.ax7=new A.a7("What does 'tiling' mean in the context of FlashAttention?",B.akf,3,"Tiling is the core mechanism: Q, K, and V are divided into blocks that fit in SRAM, and attention is computed block by block without writing the full matrix to HBM.")
B.aft=s([B.awa,B.ay5,B.ax7],t.b)
B.auz=new A.cS("SRC-FLASHATTN","FlashAttention","2022","Tri Dao et al., Stanford University and University at Buffalo","Attention can be made faster and use less memory by being IO-aware: carefully managing which data lives in the GPU's tiny fast on-chip memory versus its large slow main memory, rather than just cutting arithmetic.","Standard attention writes the full N-by-N score matrix to slow GPU memory (HBM), which makes it both slow and memory-hungry for long sequences. FlashAttention splits Q, K, and V into small blocks, loads them into fast on-chip SRAM, and computes attention block by block without ever materializing the full N-by-N matrix in HBM. It also avoids storing that matrix for the backward pass by recomputing it on-chip from a few saved statistics. The result is up to 3x end-to-end speedup on GPT-2 training and memory that scales linearly (not quadratically) with sequence length. The same idea extends to block-sparse attention, which is 2-4x faster than even FlashAttention when the attention pattern is sparse.","Before FlashAttention, long context was limited by GPU memory traffic, not just arithmetic. By never writing the full attention matrix to slow memory, it made training and running with much longer sequences practical, and its ideas became the default in major frameworks.","vLLM and Hugging Face Transformers use FlashAttention kernels on supported NVIDIA GPUs. llama.cpp has its own implementation of the same idea, switched on with the -fa (--flash-attn) option; it saves memory and usually speeds things up at long context, which is why many guides tell you to turn it on.","The formal IO-complexity proofs in Section 3.2 and Appendix C can be safely skipped; the core intuition (fewer memory copies means faster) is what matters for a practitioner.",B.anR,B.aaT,B.aft)
B.a9z=s(["Figure 1: Shows how GPU memory is split between model weights (~65%) and KV cache (~30%) on an A100, and how vLLM smooths the rapid KV cache growth curve that limits batch size.","Figure 2: Bar chart showing that existing serving systems waste 60-80% of KV cache memory, with only 20.4% to 38.2% storing actual token states.","Section 4.1: The PagedAttention algorithm explained with the block-wise attention formula and the 'Four score and seven' example of non-contiguous blocks."],t.s)
B.azn=new A.o("KV cache","Stored key and value vectors from previous tokens, needed to generate the next one.")
B.aB7=new A.o("PagedAttention","An attention mechanism that reads keys and values from scattered blocks instead of one contiguous chunk.")
B.azI=new A.o("Block table","A lookup table mapping each logical block of a request to its actual physical location in GPU memory.")
B.aC7=new A.o("Throughput","How many tokens per second the system can generate across all users combined.")
B.aBw=new A.o("Beam search","A decoding strategy that keeps the top-k most probable sequences alive at each step.")
B.adC=s([B.azn,B.aB7,B.azI,B.aC7,B.aBw],t.L)
B.akr=s(["Model weights consume too much GPU memory","The KV cache for each request wastes most of its pre-allocated memory","GPU computation is too slow for long sequences","The model must be split across multiple GPUs"],t.s)
B.ayz=new A.a7("What is the core problem PagedAttention solves?",B.akr,1,"Existing systems pre-allocate a contiguous chunk for the maximum sequence length, leaving 60-80% of that space unused.")
B.aa5=s(["In one large contiguous block per request","In fixed-size blocks that can be scattered anywhere in memory","In a separate CPU memory region","In a circular buffer that overwrites the oldest tokens"],t.s)
B.axf=new A.a7("How does PagedAttention store the KV cache?",B.aa5,1,"Blocks are allocated on demand and do not need to be adjacent, mirroring how an OS maps virtual pages to physical memory.")
B.afA=s(["2-4x higher throughput at the same latency","50% reduction in model parameter size","10x faster single-token generation","3x lower GPU power consumption"],t.s)
B.ax1=new A.a7("What is the main performance gain reported in the paper?",B.afA,0,"The abstract and evaluation sections both state vLLM improves throughput by 2-4x compared to FasterTransformer and Orca without affecting latency.")
B.a8u=s([B.ayz,B.axf,B.ax1],t.b)
B.auI=new A.cS("SRC-VLLM","vLLM","2023","Woosuk Kwon et al., UC Berkeley / Stanford / UC San Diego","The key-value cache of a running LLM can be split into small fixed-size blocks that do not need to sit next to each other in memory, just like an operating system pages virtual memory. This eliminates most of the wasted GPU space and lets you batch far more requests at once.","When serving an LLM, the key-value cache for each request grows and shrinks as tokens are generated. Existing systems pre-allocate a contiguous chunk of memory for the maximum possible sequence length, wasting most of it: only 20.4% to 38.2% of that space actually stores token states. PagedAttention splits the KV cache into fixed-size blocks that can live anywhere in GPU memory, and vLLM manages those blocks with a block table, allocating them on demand. This near-zero waste means many more requests fit in memory at the same time. In their benchmarks, vLLM delivers 2 to 4 times higher throughput than FasterTransformer and Orca at the same latency, and the gap widens with longer sequences and larger models.","Before vLLM, GPU memory was the bottleneck for serving LLMs: you could only batch a handful of requests before running out of space for the KV cache. By reclaiming the wasted memory, vLLM made it practical to serve many concurrent users on a single GPU, cutting the cost per request. It became the default inference engine for a large share of open-source LLM deployments.","vLLM is a widely used open-source serving engine with an OpenAI-compatible API; front-ends such as Open WebUI can connect to it like any other API. It shines when many requests run at once (several users, agents, batch jobs) on NVIDIA or AMD GPUs; for one person on one consumer GPU, llama.cpp-based tools are often simpler.","The distributed execution details in Section 4.6 and the kernel-level optimization tricks in Section 5.1 are implementation specifics that will not change how you use the tool.",B.a9z,B.adC,B.a8u)
B.a9S=s(["Figure 1: Side-by-side perplexity curves for OPT at 4-bit and BLOOM at 3-bit, showing where RTN collapses and GPTQ holds steady.","Table 3: Full OPT perplexity results across all model sizes at 4-bit and 3-bit; the 175B row makes the accuracy gap concrete.","Section 4, Steps 1-3: The three algorithmic insights (fixed order, lazy batch updates, Cholesky reformulation) that turn a cubic-time method into one that finishes in hours."],t.s)
B.aCs=new A.o("quantization","Reducing each weight from 16 bits to 3 or 4 bits to save memory.")
B.aDu=new A.o("post-training","Compressing a model after it is already trained, without retraining it.")
B.aA3=new A.o("perplexity","A lower-is-better score; higher means the model is more surprised by text.")
B.aAH=new A.o("Hessian","A matrix describing how sensitive the model output is to each weight.")
