B.ax3=new A.a7("Why does AWQ scale up salient channels instead of simply keeping them in FP16?",B.aj9,3,"Keeping a few channels in FP16 while the rest are INT4 creates a mixed-precision format that GPUs and CPUs handle poorly.")
B.ai7=s(["Backpropagation","A calibration set","Activation statistics","A GPU"],t.s)
B.awr=new A.a7("What does AWQ explicitly NOT rely on?",B.ai7,0,"AWQ only measures average activation magnitudes from a small calibration set; it needs no gradient updates or weight reconstruction.")
B.aag=s([B.ayL,B.ax3,B.awr],t.b)
B.auE=new A.cS("SRC-AWQ","AWQ","2023","Ji Lin et al., MIT / NVIDIA / Shanghai Jiao Tong University / Tsinghua University / MIT-IBM Watson AI Lab / UMass Amherst","Not all weights in an LLM are equally important. By looking at activation magnitudes (not weight values) to find the tiny fraction of channels that matter, and scaling those channels up before quantizing, you protect them from quantization error while keeping every weight at the same low bit width.","Running large models on a laptop or phone is hard because the weights take up too much memory. Naive 4-bit quantization destroys accuracy because it treats every weight the same. AWQ notices that about 1% of weight channels carry most of the signal, identified by how large their activations are. Instead of keeping those channels in FP16 (which confuses hardware), it scales them up before quantizing, which mathematically shrinks their relative rounding error. On OPT-6.7B at INT3, this brought perplexity from 23.54 down to 11.92. Their inference engine TinyChat turns the 4-bit savings into a measured 3.2-3.3x speedup over HuggingFace FP16 on desktop and mobile GPUs.","It showed that a few percent of weight channels, found by looking at activations, carry most of the accuracy, and that protecting them keeps 4-bit models close to full quality without any retraining. Because it needs only a small calibration set and no backpropagation, it became one of the standard ways to publish 4-bit models for GPU servers.","On Hugging Face you will find many models with \"AWQ\" in the name: 4-bit versions made with this method, loaded by vLLM, SGLang or Hugging Face Transformers on NVIDIA GPUs. GGUF files for llama.cpp, Ollama and LM Studio use llama.cpp's own quant types instead, but the same lesson (protect the weights that matter most, found with sample text) lives on in llama.cpp's importance matrix (imatrix).","The TinyChat system section (Section 4) on kernel fusion, SIMD-aware weight packing, and roofline analysis is engineering detail you do not need to understand the quantization idea itself.",B.aer,B.ad0,B.aag)
B.agq=s(["Figure 2: Side-by-side heatmaps showing key cache has a few bright outlier channels while value cache is uniform.","Table 1: The 2-bit comparison on Llama-2-13B showing that K-per-channel + V-per-token keeps accuracy near 16-bit while all other 2-bit configs collapse.","Section 3.2: The explanation of why per-token quantization of value cache gives 15x smaller error than per-channel, because attention is 84.3% sparse."],t.s)
B.aCe=new A.o("KV cache","Stored keys and values from previous tokens, avoiding attention recomputation.")
B.aAC=new A.o("Per-channel quantization","Quantizing all values within one channel together, sharing one scale.")
B.aAq=new A.o("Per-token quantization","Quantizing all values within one token together, sharing one scale.")
B.aCV=new A.o("Attention sparsity","Most attention scores are near zero; only a few tokens dominate the output.")
B.aAi=new A.o("Sliding window","A small buffer of recent tokens kept at full precision for accuracy.")
B.aj0=s([B.aCe,B.aAC,B.aAq,B.aCV,B.aAi],t.L)
B.afE=s(["Because it has a few fixed channels with very large values (outliers)","Because per-channel is always more accurate than per-token","Because the model was trained with per-channel quantization","Because value cache requires it"],t.s)
B.ay4=new A.a7("Why does key cache use per-channel quantization in KIVI?",B.afE,0,"Figure 2 and Section 3.2 show that key cache has persistent outlier channels, so per-channel quantization confines the error to those channels.")
B.ako=s(["Because value cache has fixed outlier channels","Because per-token is the only option for streaming inference","Because attention is sparse, so quantizing one token does not corrupt the output of important tokens","Because value magnitudes are always small"],t.s)
B.awt=new A.a7("Why does value cache use per-token quantization in KIVI?",B.ako,2,"Section 3.2 explains that attention sparsity (84.3%) means the output depends on only a few tokens, so per-token error confinement keeps the relative error 15x smaller.")
B.ajG=s(["It stores the entire prompt at full precision","It keeps the most recent tokens at full precision, which is crucial for hard tasks like mathematical reasoning","It replaces the quantized cache after generation is complete","It reduces the group size used for quantization"],t.s)
B.avP=new A.a7("What role does the full-precision sliding window play in KIVI?",B.ajG,1,"Section 3.3 and 4.2.1 show that the full-precision window (residual length R) is what keeps GSM8K accuracy within about 2% of the 16-bit baseline.")
B.anv=s([B.ay4,B.awt,B.avP],t.b)
B.auM=new A.cS("SRC-KIVI","KIVI","2024","Zirui Liu et al., Rice University, Texas A&M University, Stevens Institute of Technology, Carnegie Mellon University","Key cache has a few fixed channels with huge values, so it should be quantized per-channel; value cache has no such pattern, so it should be quantized per-token. This asymmetric 2-bit scheme works without any tuning.","When you run many requests or long conversations, the KV cache (the stored attention keys and values) balloons in size and slows everything down. The authors studied the actual numbers inside KV cache and found that key cache has a handful of channels with very large magnitudes, while value cache looks uniform. They built KIVI, which quantizes keys per-channel and values per-token at 2 bits, keeping a small full-precision window for the most recent tokens. On Llama-2-7B this cuts peak memory by 2.6x and enables up to 4x larger batch sizes, giving 2.35x to 3.47x throughput on real workloads. The accuracy drop is around 2% on hard tasks like GSM8K.","It showed that keys and values behave differently (keys have a few huge outlier channels, values do not) and that treating them differently lets the cache go down to 2 bits without retraining. That made the KV cache a serious target for compression, which matters more as contexts grow.","llama.cpp can store the KV cache in 8 or 4 bits (-ctk / -ctv, e.g. q8_0), and Ollama and LM Studio expose the same option; it saves a lot of memory at long context. Those are simpler schemes than KIVI's 2-bit one, but the goal is the same: the cache, not just the weights, can be squeezed.","The CUDA/Triton kernel details and the ablation on group size and residual length are implementation specifics you can skip unless you are writing your own quantization code.",B.agq,B.aj0,B.anv)
B.alU=s(["Figure 1: a visual walkthrough of one iteration, showing green accepted tokens, red rejected tokens, and blue corrections","Table 2: the measured speedup numbers for T5-XXL with T5-small, T5-base, and T5-large as draft models","Section 2.3: the speculative sampling rule that guarantees the output distribution stays identical"],t.s)
B.aAQ=new A.o("autoregressive model","A model that writes one token at a time, each depending on everything before it.")
B.aA0=new A.o("speculative execution","Doing a computation in parallel while simultaneously checking whether it was actually needed.")
B.aCW=new A.o("acceptance rate (alpha)","How often the small model's guesses match what the big model would have chosen.")
B.aBb=new A.o("output distribution","The probability pattern of which tokens the model can produce; it stays unchanged.")
B.aB2=new A.o("KV cache","Stored attention keys and values that let the model remember prior tokens without re-reading them.")
B.alK=s([B.aAQ,B.aA0,B.aCW,B.aBb,B.aB2],t.L)
B.aeB=s(["It replaces the large model entirely for easy tokens","It proposes several tokens that the large model then verifies in parallel","It fine-tunes the large model's weights before generation","It compresses the large model into fewer parameters"],t.s)
B.awi=new A.a7("What is the role of the smaller draft model in speculative decoding?",B.aeB,1,"The draft model autoregressively samples gamma guesses, and the target model evaluates all of them in a single parallel run.")
B.adK=s(["The entire generation is discarded and restarted from the beginning","The large model samples a replacement token from an adjusted distribution","The small model is asked to try a different token","The token is kept but its probability is reduced"],t.s)
B.aw7=new A.a7("What happens when the large model rejects a drafted token?",B.adK,1,"The algorithm samples from p'(x) = norm(max(0, p(x) - q(x))) to produce the corrected token.")
B.aeY=s(["It is always shorter than standard decoding","It has a higher probability of producing common words","It has the exact same distribution as the large model alone","It is always grammatically correct"],t.s)
B.awA=new A.a7("What is guaranteed about the output of speculative decoding?",B.aeY,2,"The paper proves that for any choice of draft model, the output distribution is mathematically identical to the target model's.")
B.aem=s([B.awi,B.aw7,B.awA],t.b)
B.auu=new A.cS("SRC-SPECDEC","Speculative Decoding","2022","Yaniv Leviathan et al., Google Research","A small model drafts a few tokens, and the big model checks all of them in a single parallel pass. The output is mathematically identical to what the big model would produce on its own.","Generating text from a large model is slow because every token requires a separate serial run. The authors use a much smaller model to draft several tokens at once, then run the large model in parallel to verify all the guesses in one step. It accepts the tokens that fit and samples a replacement from an adjusted distribution for the first one that does not. On T5-XXL (11B parameters), they measured speedups of 2.6x to 3.4x over the standard T5X implementation, with no change to the output distribution. The method needs no retraining, no architecture changes, and no new model training.","It turned a serial bottleneck into a parallel one, giving large models a major latency cut without touching the model or its outputs. It became a standard technique in production LLM serving, where memory bandwidth is the real bottleneck and spare compute is available.","llama.cpp supports it with a small draft model (-md / --model-draft), LM Studio has a speculative decoding setting, and vLLM and SGLang offer it for serving. Pairing a big model with a tiny one from the same family (same tokenizer) can make replies noticeably faster with exactly the same output.","The divergence definitions and theorems in Section 3 (DLK, Theorem 3.5, Theorem 3.11) are dense math that a hobbyist can safely skip.",B.alU,B.alK,B.aem)
B.a9O=s(["Figure 1: Shows the MEDUSA heads sitting on top of the model and how candidates are assembled and verified in a single step.","Table 3: Breaks down the speedup contribution of each piece: heads alone (~1.5x), adding tree attention (~1.9x), optimized tree (~2.2x), and Medusa-2 training (~2.8x).","Section 2.3.1: Explains the typical acceptance scheme that replaces rejection sampling and lets higher temperatures actually help speed."],t.s)
B.aBp=new A.o("Auto-regressive decoding","Generating text one token at a time, each one depending on all previous ones.")
B.aAI=new A.o("Speculative decoding","Guessing several future tokens ahead so the big model can verify them in one step.")
B.aBc=new A.o("Tree attention","An attention mask that lets multiple candidate continuations be checked at once in a single pass.")
B.aAx=new A.o("Draft model","A smaller model that guesses what the big model would say next.")
B.aAf=new A.o("Rejection sampling","Accepting or rejecting a candidate based on whether it matches the original model's probabilities.")
B.aex=s([B.aBp,B.aAI,B.aBc,B.aAx,B.aAf],t.L)
B.aar=s(["Extra small decoding heads on top of the model","A separate smaller draft model","Additional transformer layers in the middle","A larger vocabulary"],t.s)
B.axu=new A.a7("What does MEDUSA add to an existing LLM to speed up inference?",B.aar,0,"Medusa heads are single-layer feed-forward networks appended to the last hidden states, not a separate model.")
B.ae9=s(["The model runs out of electricity","The generated text is too long","Each token requires moving all model parameters from high-bandwidth memory to the accelerator cache","The GPU has too many unused cores"],t.s)
B.awI=new A.a7("Why is standard auto-regressive decoding a bottleneck?",B.ae9,2,"Every forward pass for a single token must transfer the full model weights, making the process memory-bandwidth-bound.")
B.afd=s(["Replace the entire model with a tree structure","Verify multiple candidate continuations simultaneously in one step","Generate tokens in random order","Compress model weights into fewer bits"],t.s)
B.avM=new A.a7("What does the tree-based attention mechanism allow MEDUSA to do?",B.afd,1,"The tree-shaped attention mask lets the model process several candidate continuations in parallel without expanding the batch size.")
B.ahU=s([B.axu,B.awI,B.avM],t.b)
B.auG=new A.cS("SRC-MEDUSA","Medusa","2024","Tianle Cai et al., Princeton University / Together AI / University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign / Carnegie Mellon University / University of Connecticut","Instead of training a separate small model to guess ahead, you bolt a few tiny extra heads onto the big model itself. Each head predicts a different future token in parallel, and the model checks all of them at once.","LLMs generate text one token at a time, and every single token requires shuffling all model parameters from high-bandwidth memory to the accelerator. That makes inference slow. The authors add a handful of small extra decoding heads on top of an existing model; each head predicts a different upcoming token. A tree-shaped attention mask lets the model verify all candidate continuations in one forward pass. MEDUSA-1 (frozen backbone) gives a 2.18x speedup on Vicuna-7B, and MEDUSA-2 (joint training) pushes that to 2.83x. No separate draft model is needed, so the approach slots cleanly into any existing serving stack.",'It removed the need to find, train and serve a separate draft model: a few small heads on the big model do the guessing. That made "guess ahead, check fast" much easier to add, and built-in guessing heads became common in newer models.',"Serving engines such as vLLM, SGLang and TensorRT-LLM support speculative decoding, including Medusa- and EAGLE-style heads that live inside the model. The same idea is behind the multi-token prediction (MTP) heads of newer models such as DeepSeek-V3, which engines use to speed up generation.","The Impact Statement on societal, environmental, and ethical considerations adds no technical insight and can be skipped safely.",B.a9O,B.aex,B.ahU)
B.acA=s(["Figure 1: the color-coded grid showing which experts fire for each token across layers 0 and 15, with gray squares marking the LRU cache (k=2).","Section 3.2: the explanation of why you cannot pre-fetch the next layer's experts in a MoE model, and how applying the next layer's gating function to the current hidden states gives a reliable guess.","Table 2: the side-by-side tokens-per-second numbers for the full algorithm versus removing pre-loading, removing the cache, and naive offloading across four GPUs."],t.s)
B.azc=new A.o("Mixture-of-Experts (MoE)","A model where only a small subset of 'expert' layers runs for each token.")
B.azF=new A.o("Offloading","Storing model weights in cheap RAM and loading them to the GPU just-in-time.")
B.aCT=new A.o("LRU cache","Keep the most recently used items; evict the least recently used when full.")
B.aCP=new A.o("Gating function","A small network that picks which experts should process the current token.")
B.aAJ=new A.o("Mixed quantization","Compress different parts of a model to different bit-widths, e.g. 4-bit attention, 2-bit experts.")
B.ao0=s([B.azc,B.azF,B.aCT,B.aCP,B.aAJ],t.L)
B.add=s(["The experts are too large to fit in PCIe transfer buffers","The model does not know which experts to load until the current layer finishes computing","The gating function requires a separate GPU pass","The experts are stored in a random order in RAM"],t.s)
B.awL=new A.a7("Why can't a MoE model use the same simple 'pre-load the next layer' trick that works for dense models?",B.add,1,"MoE layers choose their active experts based on the hidden states they receive, so the system cannot fetch the right experts until the previous layer has produced those states.")
B.ans=s(["It re-trains experts that are used frequently","It keeps the last k active experts in VRAM so they are instantly available if reused on the next token","It compresses unused experts to 1-bit to save space","It moves all experts from RAM to GPU in a fixed round-robin order"],t.s)
B.avR=new A.a7("What does the LRU expert cache actually do during token generation?",B.ans,1,"The cache holds the k most recently used experts per layer in GPU memory, so if the same expert is selected again, no RAM-to-GPU transfer is needed.")
B.afG=s(["It applies the next layer's gating function to the current layer's hidden states and loads the top-1 or top-2 predicted experts in the background","It duplicates every expert into VRAM so no loading is ever needed","It waits for the user to type the next character and pre-loads accordingly","It uses a separate small model to predict which experts will be needed"],t.s)
B.awZ=new A.a7("How does speculative expert loading work without changing the model's output?",B.afG,0,"Because transformer layers are residual, the current hidden states are a good proxy for the next layer's input, so the gating function can be run early to guess the right experts; if the guess is wrong, the correct ones are loaded later.")
B.aiO=s([B.awL,B.avR,B.awZ],t.b)
B.aun=new A.cS("SRC-MOEOFFLOAD","MoE Offloading","2023","Artyom Eliseev et al., Moscow Institute of Physics and Technology / Yandex","MoE models are too big to fit in a consumer GPU, but because only a few experts are active per token, you can keep the recently used ones cached and guess the next ones ahead of time, so the GPU never has to wait for a full layer load.","Mixtral-8x7B has 46.7B parameters, 96.6% of which are experts, and it does not fit in 12-16 GB of VRAM. Naive offloading loads the entire expert layer for every token, which is slow because interactive generation is one token at a time. The authors noticed two things: the same experts get reused across adjacent tokens, and the hidden states from one layer already predict which experts the next layer will need. They built a system that keeps a small LRU cache of recently used experts in VRAM and speculatively pre-loads the most likely next experts while the current layer is still computing. Combined with mixed quantization (4-bit for attention, 2-3 bit for experts), this runs Mixtral-8x7B-Instruct at 2-3 tokens per second on a desktop RTX 3060 or a free-tier Colab T4.","It showed that Mixtral-class MoE models could run interactively on consumer GPUs and free cloud notebooks by caching recently used experts and loading likely next experts early. Offloading experts to system RAM is now a standard way to run big MoE models at home.","llama.cpp can keep MoE expert weights in system RAM while the rest runs on the GPU (--cpu-moe, --n-cpu-moe), and its newer --moe-cache-mib option keeps a cache of experts in VRAM, the same trick this paper used. That is how people run very large MoE models on one gaming GPU plus lots of RAM.","The full quantization sweep in Section 4.2 and Table 1 (every combination of 2/3/4-bit for attention and experts) is interesting for a quantization researcher but not essential for understanding the offloading idea.",B.acA,B.ao0,B.aiO)
B.adz=s(["Figure 1: The diagram showing how PI, NTK-aware, NTK-by-parts, and YaRN relate to each other.","Section 3.2: The NTK-by-parts interpolation, where the paper explains why high-frequency dimensions should not be stretched and introduces the ramp function.","Table 1: Perplexity scores showing the s=32 model (trained on 64k data) successfully handles 128k context."],t.s)
B.aDh=new A.o("RoPE (Rotary Position Embedding)","A way to mark each token's position by rotating vectors at different speeds.")
B.azC=new A.o("Context window","The maximum number of tokens a model can read at once.")
B.aDe=new A.o("Interpolation","Rescaling position signals so the model can read longer texts.")
B.az0=new A.o("Scale factor","How many times longer the new context is versus the original.")
B.aAM=new A.o("Perplexity","A score where lower means the model is less surprised by the text.")
B.akY=s([B.aDh,B.azC,B.aDe,B.az0,B.aAM],t.L)
B.aiC=s(["Making models generate faster tokens","Allowing models to read texts longer than their training context","Reducing the total number of parameters in a model","Improving the model's translation quality"],t.s)
B.ayo=new A.a7("What is the main problem YaRN solves?",B.aiC,1,"The paper's entire goal is extending the context window of RoPE-based models beyond their pre-trained length.")
B.a9T=s(["It increases memory usage beyond GPU capacity","It makes the model forget its training data","It causes the attention mechanism to produce NaN values","It removes high-frequency positional information the model still needs"],t.s)
B.aym=new A.a7("Why does stretching all RoPE frequencies equally (Position Interpolation) fail at large scale factors?",B.a9T,3,"Section 3.1 explains that uniform scaling strips out the high-frequency components that encode relative local distances.")
B.adm=s(["It scales dimensions based on their wavelength relative to the training context length","It scales every dimension by the same fixed factor","It uses a random assignment of scaling factors per dimension","It scales only the dimensions with the highest frequency"],t.s)
B.awG=new A.a7("How does YaRN's NTK-by-parts component decide which dimensions to scale?",B.adm,0,"Section 3.2 defines a ratio r = L/lambda for each dimension and uses a ramp function to interpolate only where the wavelength exceeds the context length.")
B.aaZ=s([B.ayo,B.aym,B.awG],t.b)
B.auO=new A.cS("SRC-YARN","YaRN","2023","Bowen Peng et al., Nous Research, EleutherAI, University of Geneva","You can make a language model read much longer texts by selectively tweaking how it encodes position, without retraining it from scratch. It takes far less data and compute than earlier methods.","LLMs trained with Rotary Position Embeddings (RoPE) break down on texts longer than their training context. The authors show that stretching every RoPE frequency equally, as Position Interpolation does, destroys the high-frequency positional signals the model still needs. YaRN fixes this by scaling only the low-frequency dimensions and leaving high-frequency ones untouched, then adding a small temperature tweak to the attention logits. The result is a model that reads 128k tokens after just 400 fine-tuning steps on roughly 0.1% of the original pre-training data, using 10x fewer tokens and 2.5x fewer steps than prior methods. The s=32 model, trained only on 64k-token chunks, successfully extrapolates to 128k context.","Before YaRN, extending a model's context window meant expensive retraining or accepting degraded quality, which made long-context models impractical for most labs. YaRN turned context extension into a cheap, few-hundred-step fine-tune, and its compatibility with Flash Attention 2 meant it could be adopted immediately by the open-source community.","Qwen3's model cards tell you to switch on YaRN to go from 32,768 to 131,072 tokens of context; in llama.cpp that is the --rope-scaling yarn option. Many long-context versions of open models were made with YaRN.","The complex-number derivations in Section 2.1 and the NTK-aware base-change algebra in Appendix A.2 are for the curious, not the essential.",B.adz,B.akY,B.aaZ)
B.air=s([B.auz,B.auI,B.aus,B.auE,B.auM,B.auu,B.auG,B.aun,B.auO],t.v5)
B.aue=new A.l5("Running models fast",B.air)
B.alu=s(["Figure 1: The diagram of the MoE layer showing how a router assigns each input vector to 2 of the 8 experts and the output is the weighted sum of those two.","Table 2: The head-to-head benchmark table where Mixtral beats or matches Llama 2 70B on nearly every task while using 5x fewer active parameters.","Section 5 (Routing analysis): Shows that experts do not specialise by topic (math, biology, etc.) but by syntactic role, and that consecutive tokens often get the same expert."],t.s)
B.aA1=new A.o("Mixture of Experts (MoE)","A layer with several small sub-networks; only a few are used per token.")
B.aB1=new A.o("Router","A small network that picks which two experts should handle the current token.")
B.aAt=new A.o("Active parameters","The 13B weights actually used for a token, not the full 47B model size.")
B.aD0=new A.o("Sparse","Only a small fraction of the model's weights are touched for each token.")
B.afY=s([B.aA1,B.aB1,B.aAt,B.aD0],t.L)
B.a8x=s(["All 8 experts process the token and their outputs are averaged","A router selects 2 of the 8 experts and their outputs are combined","The token is processed by the single most relevant expert","The token is split into 8 pieces, one per expert"],t.s)
B.avQ=new A.a7("What happens at each layer for every token in Mixtral?",B.a8x,1,"The paper states that a router network selects two experts to process the current state and combine their outputs additively.")
B.ajJ=s(["Only 2 of the 8 expert blocks are activated for each token","The model skips every other layer during inference","It uses a shorter context window than other models","The experts run in parallel on separate GPUs"],t.s)
B.awn=new A.a7("Why does Mixtral need less compute per token than a dense model with 47B parameters?",B.ajJ,0,"The paper explains that increasing n while keeping K fixed increases parameter count while keeping computational cost effectively constant.")
B.afD=s(["47B is the training size; 13B is the inference size","47B is the number of layers; 13B is the number of attention heads","47B is the vocabulary size; 13B is the context length","47B is the total parameters in the model; 13B is the parameters actually used per token"],t.s)
B.ay3=new A.a7("What is the difference between Mixtral's 47B and 13B parameter counts?",B.afD,3,"The paper distinguishes the sparse parameter count (47B, total model size) from the active parameter count (13B, parameters used to process an individual token).")
B.ahS=s([B.avQ,B.awn,B.ay3],t.b)
B.auA=new A.cS("SRC-MIXTRAL","Mixtral","2024","Albert Q. Jiang et al., Mistral AI",'Instead of running every parameter for every token, the model has 8 small "expert" blocks per layer and only activates 2 of them, so it gets big-model knowledge while doing a fraction of the compute.',"Dense models use all their weights for every single token, which is slow and expensive. Mistral AI built Mixtral 8x7B by replacing the feedforward block in each of its 32 layers with 8 separate expert blocks; a small router network picks 2 of those 8 for each token. The model has 47B total parameters but only 13B are active per token. On standard benchmarks it matches or beats Llama 2 70B, scoring 70.6% on MMLU and 74.4% on GSM8K versus 69.9% and 69.6% for the 70B model. The Instruct version scored 8.30 on MT-Bench, outperforming GPT-3.5 Turbo and Claude-2.1 in human evaluations.","It proved that an open-weight model could deliver near-70B quality while only computing 13B worth of work per token, making strong models practical on consumer hardware. The Apache 2.0 release and vLLM integration lowered the barrier for anyone wanting to self-host a capable model.","Mixtral was one of the first open mixture-of-experts models people ran at home with llama.cpp, Ollama and LM Studio. Because only 2 of 8 experts work on each token, it generates about as fast as a 13B dense model while knowing much more, the same trade-off you get from today's MoE models.","The bias benchmarks in Section 3.3 and the multilingual tables in Table 4 are interesting context but not needed to understand how the MoE architecture works.",B.alu,B.afY,B.ahS)
B.abH=s(["Figure 1: the overview diagram showing how the selection mechanism works and why it requires a hardware-aware algorithm","Section 3.1: the two synthetic tasks (selective copying and induction heads) that motivated the whole idea","Table 3: zero-shot benchmark results showing Mamba outperforming Pythia and RWKV at the same size"],t.s)
B.aD7=new A.o("State Space Model (SSM)","A sequence model that updates a hidden state step by step, like a running memory.")
B.aBo=new A.o("Selective (selection mechanism)","The model picks what to keep or discard based on the actual content it reads.")
B.aAK=new A.o("Linear time scaling","Cost grows in proportion to sequence length, not length squared.")
B.aB3=new A.o("KV cache","The stored context a Transformer keeps so it can attend back to earlier tokens.")
B.ajW=s([B.aD7,B.aBo,B.aAK,B.aB3],t.L)
B.aeK=s(["The model's internal parameters become functions of the input, so it can choose what to remember or forget per token","The model uses complex numbers in its hidden state for better audio performance","The model replaces all linear layers with convolutional layers","The model adds a second attention head to each block"],t.s)
B.axc=new A.a7("What is the core change that makes Mamba different from earlier state space models?",B.aeK,0,"Making SSM parameters input-dependent is the central contribution; it lets the model selectively propagate or forget information.")
B.a99=s(["It is significantly worse than same-size Transformers","It is slightly better but only on short sequences","It outperforms same-size Transformers and matches Transformers twice its size","It only matches same-size Transformers on pretraining perplexity"],t.s)
B.awM=new A.a7("How does Mamba-3B compare to Transformers of the same size on language tasks?",B.a99,2,"The paper states Mamba-3B outperforms same-size Transformers and matches Transformers twice its size in both pretraining and downstream evaluation.")
