B.ayr=new A.a7("What does the Mamba architecture NOT contain?",B.a8m,0,"Mamba combines the SSM and MLP into a single homogeneous block and uses no attention at all.")
B.aj2=s([B.axc,B.awM,B.ayr],t.b)
B.aul=new A.cS("SRC-MAMBA","Mamba","2023","Albert Gu et al., Carnegie Mellon University / Princeton University","Instead of applying the same fixed rules to every token, the model decides for each one whether to remember it or let it go, based on the content.","Transformers are strong at language but slow on long sequences because their cost grows quadratically with context length. Earlier fast alternatives called state space models were too rigid: they used the same fixed rules for every token and could not tell important content from noise. The authors made the model's internal parameters depend on the input, so it can selectively focus on relevant tokens and ignore the rest. They also designed a hardware-aware algorithm that keeps this selective computation fast on modern GPUs. The result is a model with no attention and no separate MLP blocks that runs 5x faster than Transformers and matches a Transformer twice its size in quality.","It showed a strong language model without attention, whose memory per step does not grow with the length of the text. That started a wave of attention-free and hybrid designs aimed at very long contexts.","Pure Mamba models are rare, but its ideas live on in hybrid models that mix Mamba-style layers with a few attention layers to save memory at long context. llama.cpp can run Mamba-family layers, so some of these hybrids run at home like any other GGUF.","The formal SSM math in Section 2 (discretization formulas, LTI definitions) and the GPU memory-hierarchy details in Section 3.3 are dense and not needed to understand the core idea.",B.abH,B.ajW,B.aj2)
B.aaF=s(["Figure 1: The bar chart comparing DeepSeek-V3 against GPT-4o, Claude-3.5-Sonnet, Qwen2.5-72B, and Llama-3.1-405B across six benchmarks.","Section 2.1.2: The explanation of how the per-expert bias term is nudged up or down each training step to keep loads even without adding a penalty loss.","Table 1: The training cost breakdown showing 2664K GPU hours for pre-training, 119K for context extension, 5K for post-training, totaling 2788K hours at $5.576M."],t.s)
B.aCD=new A.o("Mixture-of-Experts (MoE)","A model where only a small subset of 'expert' layers fires for each token.")
B.aBt=new A.o("Multi-head Latent Attention (MLA)","An attention trick that compresses keys and values to shrink the KV cache.")
B.aCB=new A.o("Load balancing","Keeping every expert roughly equally busy so none sits idle or drowns in work.")
B.aAF=new A.o("Multi-token prediction (MTP)","Training the model to forecast several future tokens at once, not just the next one.")
B.azO=new A.o("Speculative decoding","Drafting tokens with a small module that the main model then verifies, speeding up generation.")
B.ad9=s([B.aCD,B.aBt,B.aCB,B.aAF,B.azO],t.L)
B.akU=s(["671B","37B","14.8 trillion","2048"],t.s)
B.ayy=new A.a7("How many parameters does DeepSeek-V3 activate per token?",B.akU,1,"The model has 671B total parameters but only 37B are active for each token it processes.")
B.ag4=s(["Adds a penalty loss to the training objective","Drops tokens routed to overloaded experts","Adjusts a per-expert bias term each training step","Uses a separate monitor model to track expert loads"],t.s)
B.awk=new A.a7("What does the auxiliary-loss-free load balancing strategy actually do?",B.ag4,2,"A bias term is nudged up for underloaded experts and down for overloaded ones, affecting only routing, not the gating value.")
B.aeh=s(["To densify training signals and improve model performance","To speed up inference via speculative decoding","To reduce the total parameter count","To replace the standard next-token objective entirely"],t.s)
B.avY=new A.a7("What is the primary reason DeepSeek-V3 uses multi-token prediction during training?",B.aeh,0,"The MTP objective is a training-time technique that strengthens the model; the speculative decoding use at inference is a secondary bonus.")
B.afB=s([B.ayy,B.awk,B.avY],t.b)
B.aur=new A.cS("SRC-DEEPSEEKV3","DeepSeek-V3","2024","DeepSeek-AI","A 671-billion-parameter mixture-of-experts model that uses only 37B parameters per token, trained with FP8 and careful engineering for a reported $5.576M of GPU time (the final run only), and competitive with the best closed models of its day.","Open-source models were falling behind closed-source ones, and training large models was prohibitively expensive. DeepSeek built a Mixture-of-Experts model with 671B total parameters but only 37B active per token, using Multi-head Latent Attention for efficient inference and a new auxiliary-loss-free load balancing trick to keep all experts busy without hurting quality. They also added a multi-token prediction training objective that densifies learning signals. Trained on 14.8 trillion tokens on 2048 H800 GPUs, the model scored 90.2 on MATH-500 and 75.9 on MMLU-Pro, matching GPT-4o and Claude-3.5-Sonnet on several benchmarks. The entire training run cost 2.788M GPU hours ($5.576M) with no loss spikes or rollbacks.","It showed that an open-weight model trained on a modest budget could rival the best closed models, and it made several techniques mainstream: FP8 training at scale, load balancing without an auxiliary loss, and multi-token prediction. Its successor R1 was built on top of it.","Quantized DeepSeek-V3 and R1 GGUFs run in llama.cpp (and tools built on it) on machines with a lot of memory; because only 37B parameters are read per token, they generate far faster than a dense model of the same size. Its multi-token prediction (MTP) heads are used for speculative decoding by serving engines such as vLLM and SGLang.","Section 3 (Infrastructures) covers FP8 quantization internals, DualPipe scheduling, and hardware design suggestions that are systems-engineering details and will not change how you run or fine-tune the model.",B.aaF,B.ad9,B.afB)
B.amz=s([B.auA,B.aul,B.aur],t.v5)
B.auc=new A.l5("New architectures",B.amz)
B.akE=s(["Figure 1: The two-panel summary showing compute-optimal scaling beating best-of-N with 4x less compute, and the FLOPs-matched comparison against a 14x larger model.","Section 3.2: How they estimate question difficulty by binning the model's pass@1 rate from 2048 samples into five quantiles, and why model-predicted difficulty works in practice.","Figure 9: The tradeoff curves showing when test-time compute beats pretraining and when it does not, across difficulty levels and inference-to-pretraining token ratios."],t.s)
B.aAL=new A.o("Test-time compute","Extra computation a model uses while answering, beyond generating one response.")
B.aA4=new A.o("Best-of-N","Generate N answers in parallel, then pick the best one.")
B.aCp=new A.o("Process reward model (PRM)","A model that scores each step of a solution, not just the final answer.")
B.aBu=new A.o("Compute-optimal scaling","Choosing the right test-time strategy per question based on its difficulty.")
