B.agA=s([B.aAL,B.aA4,B.aCp,B.aBu,B.azW],t.L)
B.aoM=s(["Always uses more parallel search regardless of difficulty","Picks the right test-time method per prompt based on estimated difficulty","Trains a larger model to handle hard questions","Replaces the base model with a verifier-only model"],t.s)
B.awC=new A.a7("What does the compute-optimal strategy actually do?",B.aoM,1,"The strategy assigns sequential revision to easy questions and parallel search to hard ones, based on a difficulty estimate.")
B.aka=s(["Easy questions where the model already gets it right","Medium-difficulty questions","The hardest questions, where the model is outside its capabilities","All questions equally"],t.s)
B.ay1=new A.a7("On which questions does test-time compute provide very little benefit?",B.aka,2,"The paper shows that on the most difficult problems (difficulty bins 4-5), no test-time method makes meaningful progress and pretraining is preferable.")
B.aaK=s(["The 14x larger model always wins","The smaller model with test-time compute can outperform the 14x larger model","Both models perform identically","The smaller model only wins on hard questions"],t.s)
B.ax8=new A.a7("In the FLOPs-matched evaluation, what did the authors find on easy and medium questions?",B.aaK,1,"When the inference-to-pretraining token ratio is low, test-time compute with the smaller model beats the 14x larger model on easy and medium questions.")
B.ams=s([B.awC,B.ay1,B.ax8],t.b)
B.aut=new A.cS("SRC-TTC","Compute-Optimal Scaling","2024","Charlie Snell et al., UC Berkeley / Google DeepMind","Instead of spending all your extra compute the same way on every question, you should pick the right strategy per prompt: easy questions get iterative self-revision, hard questions get parallel search. This adaptive allocation beats a fixed best-of-N approach by using up to 4x less compute.","The problem: when you give a model more compute at test time, how do you spend it most effectively? The authors fine-tuned PaLM 2-S* on the MATH benchmark (12k train, 500 test questions) to do two things: iteratively revise its own answers, and verify solution steps with a process reward model. They found that the best strategy depends on question difficulty: easy questions benefit from sequential revision, hard questions benefit from parallel search. Their compute-optimal strategy, which picks the right approach per prompt, outperforms a fixed best-of-N baseline using 4x less compute. In a FLOPs-matched comparison, a smaller model with test-time compute can outperform a 14x larger model on easy and medium questions, though on the hardest problems pretraining still wins.","It reframed the scaling question: you do not always need a bigger model. Spending FLOPs at inference time, adaptively per prompt, can substitute for pretraining on many real-world tasks. This pointed toward a future where models are smaller but think longer, rather than endlessly larger.",'Reasoning models that "think" longer before answering are spending test-time compute. You can also do the simplest version yourself: generate several answers and pick the best one, or let the model check and revise its own answer.',"The detailed beam-search and lookahead-search algorithm descriptions in Section 5.2 are implementation details you can safely skip; the key takeaway is that search helps on hard problems but over-optimizes on easy ones.",B.akE,B.agA,B.ams)
B.agF=s(["Figure 1: Two panels showing AIME accuracy climbing from 15.6% to 77.9% and response length growing steadily over 10,400 training steps.","Table 2: The 'aha moment' where an intermediate model starts saying 'wait' and rethinking its approach mid-solution.","Section 2.2: How the reward works in plain terms \u2014 a binary correctness check on the final answer plus a small format bonus, with no neural reward model."],t.s)
