B.axY=new A.a7("Why did the authors skip the supervised fine-tuning step before RL?",B.acO,1,"They believed that constraining the model to replicate human thought processes would prevent it from discovering superior, non-human-like reasoning pathways.")
B.aaN=s([B.axq,B.ay6,B.axY],t.b)
B.auB=new A.cS("SRC-DEEPSEEKR1","DeepSeek-R1","2025","Daya Guo et al., DeepSeek-AI","Give a model only a right-or-wrong score on its final answer, and it will figure out how to think on its own, without being shown any human examples of reasoning.","LLMs were trained to reason by copying thousands of human-written step-by-step solutions, which is expensive and caps performance at human level. The authors skipped that step entirely: they took DeepSeek-V3-Base and trained it with reinforcement learning (GRPO), rewarding the model only when its final answer was correct on math, coding, and STEM problems. During training, AIME 2024 accuracy jumped from 15.6% to 77.9%, and the model spontaneously started double-checking its work, backtracking, and trying alternative approaches. No one taught it those behaviors; they emerged from the reward signal alone. To fix readability and language mixing, they built a multi-stage version (DeepSeek-R1) that adds supervised fine-tuning and preference-based RL on top. They also distilled the reasoning into smaller, publicly released models.","It proved that a reliable answer-checker and enough compute can replace expensive human annotation for teaching reasoning. The emergent behaviors (self-verification, reflection) were not programmed in; they grew out of the reward structure. The distilled smaller models made strong step-by-step reasoning available to people running models at home.","DeepSeek-R1 and its smaller distilled versions (built on Qwen and Llama models) are on Hugging Face and run in llama.cpp, Ollama and LM Studio; you see the long thinking section before the answer. GRPO, the reinforcement-learning method used here, is available in fine-tuning libraries such as Hugging Face TRL and Unsloth.","The GRPO objective function (equations 1-3) and the PPO comparison in Appendix A.3 are for the algorithmically curious; the core idea is far simpler than the math.",B.agF,B.alW,B.aaN)
