B.ZC=new A.fX("F32","Plain 32-bit numbers \u2014 the most detail any weight can get. Training math lives here; you almost never ship a model like this.")
B.Zo=new A.fX("F16 / FP16",'16-bit "half precision" \u2014 one of the two usual shapes a finished model is released in (the other is BF16). Two bytes per number.')
B.Zy=new A.fX("BF16",'B = "brain float": a 16-bit number that keeps the huge range of 32-bit floats but less detail. The everyday training format of modern AI.')
B.ZA=new A.fX("FP8","8-bit floats \u2014 used inside the fastest new datacenter chips for the math itself.")
B.Zs=new A.fX("NF4","A special 4-bit ruler whose 16 marks are spaced for bell-curve-shaped weights \u2014 which real model weights tend to be (the trick from the QLoRA work). Better placement beats evenly spaced marks.")
B.Zq=new A.fX("Q8_0",'8-bit quantization: half the size of F16 and, in practice, quality you can\'t tell apart. The "_0" means a simple symmetric block layout.')
B.ZB=new A.fX("Q5 / Q4 / Q3 / Q2","The number is simply the bits each weight gets. Plain (_0/_1) versions are the old, simple block layouts.")
B.Zv=new A.fX("_0 vs _1","How much note-taking each 32-weight block does: _0 stores one shared scale; _1 also stores a shared starting point (the block's minimum), so it costs a few more bits and buys a little more accuracy.")
B.Zu=new A.fX("Q4_K / Q5_K / Q6_K","k-quants: the smarter packing generation. Blocks of 32 are grouped into super-blocks with better shared notes, so a Q4_K file keeps far more of the model than the old Q4_0 at the same size. M/L variants spend a few extra bits on the most-important layers.")
B.Zt=new A.fX("_S / _M / _L","How much extra care the important layers get: S = small boost, M = medium (the default most people pick), L = large boost \u2014 a bit bigger, a bit better.")
B.Zx=new A.fX("IQ1_S \u2026 IQ4_XS",'i-quants: the newest generation. Every weight is picked from a tiny built-in dictionary of allowed values instead of a ruler \u2014 usually steered by an "importance matrix" (a test run that finds which weights matter most; required for the tiny IQ1/IQ2 sizes, optional above). Suffixes: XXS is the tightest squeeze for a bit count, then XS, S, M, L \u2014 and NL means non-linear: steps that are deliberately unevenly spaced.')
B.Zr=new A.fX("UD-\u2026_XL",'Unsloth Dynamic: a per-model recipe that hands the most sensitive layers a higher precision while the rest take the hard squeeze. The "_XL" is the biggest dose of that protection.')
B.Zp=new A.fX("GPTQ","A different quantizing recipe (outside GGUF): round the weights one by one, and each time, nudge the remaining weights to cancel the error just made.")
B.Zz=new A.fX("AWQ","Another recipe: first find the few weights the model actually leans hardest on during real use, and shield those from aggressive rounding.")
B.Zw=new A.fX("EXL2","Another carton (file format) for quantized weights, like GGUF \u2014 built for fast loading on GPUs by a different project.")
