B.asf={s1_intro:0,s1_storage:1,s1_ram:2,s1_memory_types:3,s1_cpu_gpu:4,s1_matmul:5,s1_precision:6,s1_links:7,s1_too_big:8,s1_server:9,s1_rack:10,s1_datacenter:11,s1_power_cooling:12,s1_real_sites:13,s1_data_scale:14,s2_intro:15,s2_decoder:16,s2_library:17,s2_cleaning:18,s2_tokens:19,s2_empty_brain:20,s2_attention:21,s2_guess:22,s2_loss:23,s2_backprop:24,s2_descent:25,s2_train_it:26,s2_scale_up:27,s2_parallel:28,s2_things_break:29,s2_testing:30,s2_post_training:31,s2_safety:32,s2_scaling_laws:33,s2_big_small:34,s2_making_small:35,s2_memory_budget:36,s2_step_hardware:37,s3_intro:38,s3_tokenize:39,s3_embeddings:40,s3_tiny_model:41,s3_sampling:42,s3_loop:43,s3_at_home:44,s3_quants:45,s3_frontier:46,s3_serving:47,s3_beyond_text:48,s3_timeline:49,s3_robots:50,s3_science:51,s3_answer:52,finale:53,e_good_bad:54,e_asking:55,e_privacy:56,e_fakes:57,e_school_work:58,e_which:59,e_model_size:60,e_at_home:61,e_bias:62,e_advice:63,e_ai_text:64,e_access:65,l_will_it_fit:66,l_runtimes:67,l_quant_pick:68,l_kv_cache:69,l_moe_home:70,l_speed_tricks:71,l_frontends:72,l_home_rig:73,l_split_gpus:74,l_safe_server:75,l_speech_home:76,l_which_custom:77,l_lora:78,l_datasets:79,l_merging:80,l_steering:81,l_notes_helper:82,l_tools_mcp:83,l_inside:84,l_paper_anatomy:85,l_paper_shelf:86,l_sota_models:87,l_sota_hardware:88,l_sota_techniques:89,l_leaderboards:90,l_this_year:91,l_who_makes:92,l_jobs:93,l_keep_up:94,e_agents:95,e_thinking:96,e_memory:97,e_voice:98,e_energy:99,e_pictures:100}
B.af3=s(["Why is the sky blue? Our question is now a glowing packet of [[bit]]s, and it needs a body to live in.","Let's peek inside. Tap each part of this machine and I'll show you what it does. We're building the mind, one piece at a time.","Every [[model]]\u2014the trained brain that will explain the sky\u2014needs a physical home. This cut-away shows the rooms where the thinking actually happens."],t.s)
B.a5X=new A.aY(B.af3,"A thought doesn't float in the air; it lives in [[bit]]s on chips, and those chips need roads, shelves, and power to actually think.")
B.akV=s(["Your [[hard drive]] is a spinning warehouse. Finding a file takes a few to 10 milliseconds\u2014like walking to the very back of a huge storage facility.","The [[SSD]] is the instant shelf. It answers in about a tenth of a millisecond\u2014fifty to a hundred times faster than the old drive.","Hit **Load the model** and watch the race. Same 16 GB file, four drives: a typical [[hard drive]] moves about 200 MB every second, while the fastest NVMe [[SSD]] moves 14,800 MB/s."],t.s)
B.a79=new A.aY(B.akV,"A [[hard drive]] must physically move an arm to find your data\u2014up to 10 ms. An [[SSD]] has no moving parts and answers in ~100 \xb5s\u2014a tenth of a millisecond.")
B.age=s(["Think of [[RAM]] as your desk. It's where the active papers live\u2014fast to grab, but it clears the moment the power goes off.","Each memory channel is a lane, and more lanes mean more [[bandwidth]]. One DDR5-5600 channel moves 44.8 GB/s; a 12-channel server runs faster DDR5-6400 lanes at 51.2 GB/s each, so 12 \xd7 51.2 hits 614 GB/s.","Slide the channel count and watch the [[bandwidth]] bar grow. Then tap to compare: [[RAM]] answers in ~80\u2013100 ns, while an [[SSD]] takes ~100 \xb5s (100,000 ns), about a thousand times slower."],t.s)
B.a62=new A.aY(B.age,"[[RAM]] is your desk: fast but it forgets everything when power drops. The [[SSD]] is the shelf you reach for when the desk overflows.")
B.acy=s(["[[Cache]] is your pocket\u2014about 1 nanosecond to reach. [[HBM]] is a skyscraper of memory chips stacked right beside the GPU, on the same package.","[[GDDR6X]] runs up to 1,008 GB/s on a top gaming card. [[HBM]] on an H200 hits 4.8 TB/s\u2014nearly five times faster.","Pick a memory type in the race and watch it stream model weights to the chip. The leaderboard shows real [[bandwidth]] numbers on a log scale."],t.s)
B.a6X=new A.aY(B.acy,"The closer memory sits to the chip, the faster it is\u2014[[Cache]] at 1 ns, [[HBM]] stacked beside the GPU like a tower of sticky notes.")
B.aeD=s(["A [[CPU]] is a few [[core]]s doing complex things one at a time\u2014like a handful of master chefs. A [[GPU]] is an army: 16,896 [[CUDA cores|cuda core]] on an H100, plus 528 [[tensor cores|tensor core]] built just for multiplying grids.","Hit **Showdown** and watch. The [[CPU]] fills a 24\xd724 grid a few cells at a time. The [[GPU]] fills whole blocks in one swing.","For [[matrix multiplication]], the H100 is roughly 100 to 300 times faster than a desktop [[CPU]]. That's the difference between a chef and a kitchen crew."],t.s)
B.a7e=new A.aY(B.aeD,"A [[CPU]] is a few master chefs handling complex recipes. A [[GPU]] is thousands of cooks doing the same simple step, all at the same time.")
B.aiS=s(["Every [[neural network]] runs on one trick: [[Matrix multiplication]]. Multiply a row by a column, add the results. That's the whole engine.","Tap the result cells and do the math yourself. Each cell is three multiplications added together\u2014a handful of [[FLOP]]s.","Now scale it up. A 405-billion-parameter model does about **810 billion** of these multiply-adds for every single [[token]] you read."],t.s)
B.a72=new A.aY(B.aiS,"Every [[token]] the AI outputs comes from billions of tiny multiply-and-add steps\u2014the same 3\xd73 trick, repeated at a massive scale.")
B.a7N=s(["[[Precision]] is how fine your ruler is. FP32 gives 32 [[bit]]s of detail. FP8 gives 8. FP4 gives 4. Coarser means smaller files.","Slide the bits and watch \u03c0 get rounder. A 70B [[model]] in BF16 (a 16-bit format made for AI) needs 140 GB; in FP8 it needs just 70 GB.","It's like [[quantization]]\u2014compressing a photo until it's small but still recognisable. You lose a little sharpness, but the picture stays."],t.s)
B.a5W=new A.aY(B.a7N,"Lower [[precision]] means fewer [[bit]]s per number, which shrinks the [[model]] and speeds up compute\u2014but the answer gets a little less exact.")
B.adg=s(["Parts talk to each other over [[bandwidth]] roads. [[PCIe]] is the public highway\u201464 GB/s each way on Gen 5. [[NVLink]] is a private express lane: 1.8 TB/s per [[GPU]].","[[InfiniBand]] is the [[datacenter]]'s nervous system\u2014400 to 800 Gb/s between machines. Tap a road to see how many cars it can carry.","The wider the road, the more data flows. But the first car's arrival time\u2014[[Latency]]\u2014matters just as much as the lane width."],t.s)
B.a6h=new A.aY(B.adg,"[[Bandwidth]] is how wide the road is; [[latency]] is how long until the first car arrives. Both matter, and the wrong road can bottleneck your whole machine.")
B.aia=s(["One H100 [[GPU]] has 80 GB of memory. A 70B [[model]] stored at 2 bytes per [[parameter]] needs 140 GB, already more than one chip can hold.","And [[training]] is even hungrier. Adam needs about 16 bytes per [[parameter]], so a 405B [[model]] wants ~6.5 TB before you even start.","Slide the [[model]] size and [[precision]]. Watch how many [[GPUs|gpu]] you need just to hold the weights, and about eight times as much to train it."],t.s)
B.a67=new A.aY(B.aia,"A 405B [[model]] at 2 bytes per knob needs 810 GB, at least eleven 80 GB [[GPUs|gpu]] just to hold it. [[Training]] needs about eight times that for gradients and optimizer notes.")
B.amg=s(["A [[server]] is a box of eight [[GPU]]s, glued together with [[NVLink]] switches. The DGX B200 packs 1,440 GB of [[HBM]] and 64 TB/s of [[bandwidth]].","Tap the parts. The [[CPU]]s are the master chefs; the NICs are the door to the outside world.","Power draw: about 14 kW for this box. That's about as much as twelve average U.S. homes use."],t.s)
B.a60=new A.aY(B.amg,"One [[server]] is eight [[GPU]]s sharing a table, talking through [[NVLink]] at 1.8 TB/s each. It's less like a desktop and more like a small factory.")
B.amK=s(["A [[rack]] is a tall cabinet of [[server]]s. The NVL72 goes further: 72 [[GPU]]s act as **one** machine, linked by 130 TB/s of [[NVLink]].","It runs on [[liquid cooling]]\u2014cold water flows through metal plates sitting right on the chips. Water carries about 3,500 times more heat than the same volume of air.","Toggle **air vs liquid** and compare. In air mode the fans roar and heat shimmers; in liquid mode the coolant does the work quietly."],t.s)
B.a5T=new A.aY(B.amK,"72 [[GPU]]s in one [[rack]] are wired together by [[NVLink]] so they can talk like one chip, sharing 13.4 TB of [[HBM]]. It's not a stack of separate [[server]]s\u2014it's one big brain.")
B.ak7=s(["Zoom out. The [[rack]] sits in a row, then a hall, then a campus with [[cooling towers|cooling tower]]. This is the [[datacenter]]\u2014where your sky question gets answered.","Turn the dial from 8 to 1,000,000 [[GPU]]s. Watch the [[megawatt]] numbers climb, the water flow grow, the floor area expand.","One [[megawatt]] of power runs about 830 U.S. homes. A million [[GPU]]s need far more than that."],t.s)
B.a6x=new A.aY(B.ak7,"A [[datacenter]] is a factory for [[compute]]. Scale it up and you're not building computers\u2014you're building a power plant, a cooling system, and a water loop.")
B.amI=s(["Every [[bit]] you read costs energy. The grid feeds a substation, the substation feeds backup power systems (UPS), and those feed the [[racks|rack]].","The heat flows the other way: chips \u2192 coolant \u2192 chillers \u2192 [[cooling towers|cooling tower]]. Tap each stage to see the water and the watts.","[[PUE]] is the overhead score. Industry average: 1.54. That means for every 1 W of [[compute]], you're burning 0.54 W on cooling, lights and power losses."],t.s)
B.a7d=new A.aY(B.amI,"[[PUE]] scores the overhead: a building with PUE 1.5 draws 1.5 W in total for every 1 W of [[compute]]. And the heat needs water to carry it away\u2014about 1.8 litres for every kWh used, on average.")
B.ab0=s(["These aren't science projects. xAI's Colossus in Memphis packed ~100,000 [[GPU]]s online in 122 days. Colossus 2 followed with ~110,000 more in 91 days.","Stargate in Abilene: 450,000+ [[GPU]]s, 1.2 GW\u2014enough power for a million four-bedroom homes. Meta trained Llama 3 on up to 16,000 [[GPU]]s.","Swipe the cards. Each one is a real site with real numbers, built on a deadline. The scale is hard to feel, but the [[megawatt]] numbers make it real: the biggest sites draw as much power as a city."],t.s)
B.a6U=new A.aY(B.ab0,"These sites draw hundreds of [[megawatts|megawatt]], and their power draw can swing by tens of megawatts in an instant when thousands of [[GPUs|gpu]] pause together. The grid has to absorb that.")
B.ao6=s(["How much data fills these halls? One monthly snapshot of the web is ~2.3 billion pages\u2014about 398 TiB of text.","Slide from [[token]]s to bytes to books to **years of reading**. A [[dataset]] isn't a book; it's a library with no end.","One web snapshot (~398 TiB) would fill more than a dozen of the biggest [[hard drives|hard drive]]. Models learn from text filtered out of dozens of snapshots like that; after cleaning, about 15 trillion [[token]]s are left."],t.s)
B.a6u=new A.aY(B.ao6,"A [[dataset]] is the library the [[model]] reads. Scale it up and it's not a book\u2014it's a city's worth of text.")
B.ab5=s(["You know how a student reads the textbook, then takes the test? That's [[Training]]. And [[Inference]] is the test itself\u2014the job.","Let's follow our question. At school, the model reads trillions of words. At work, it just answers your [[prompt]].","Tap the toggle and watch the table animate. School is slow and messy. The job is fast and focused."],t.s)
B.a6V=new A.aY(B.ab5,"Training is going to school; inference is doing the job. One builds the [[model]]; the other uses it.")
B.a7U=s(["Sixteen words you'll meet on the trip sound fancy. Each one is just a plain idea in a costume.","Flip a card. The engineering word is on the front. On the back, I'll tell you what it actually means.","Flip all sixteen and watch the counter climb. Every word also lives in the Glossary on the home screen, for later."],t.s)
B.a69=new A.aY(B.a7U,"Every big AI word is just a small idea in plain clothes. [[Token]] is a word piece; [[Loss]] is a wrongness score; [[Backpropagation]] is passing the blame backward.")
B.abR=s(['Before the model answers "Why is the sky blue?", it has to read the world. That\'s the [[dataset]]\u2014the library.',"Tap each source\u2014web pages, books, code, Wikipedia, papers. They all pour into one funnel.","The web part of the library, a cleaned collection called FineWeb, holds 15 trillion [[tokens|token]] from 96 web snapshots. Reading that at 250 words a minute, 8 hours a day, would take about 250,000 years."],t.s)
B.a7f=new A.aY(B.abR,"The [[dataset]] is the library the model reads. Its size and mix shape everything it can know.")
B.a89=s(["Raw web text is messy. Ads, spam, copy-pasted blogs, someone's phone number.","We run it through filters like a washing machine. Language, quality, dedupe, safety, strip personal info.","Tap each filter and watch how much of the text survives. What's left is what the model actually reads."],t.s)
B.a6r=new A.aY(B.a89,"Cleaning the [[dataset]] isn't optional. Junk in, junk out.")
B.ac9=s(["The model doesn't read words. It reads tiny pieces called [[tokens|token]]\u2014about 4 characters each, or three-quarters of a word.","The [[tokenizer]] is the word chopper. Chopping words into pieces like this took off in 2016; GPT-2's [[vocabulary]] held 50,257 tokens.","Now play the merge game. Tap the most frequent pair to glue letters into tokens."],t.s)
B.a5N=new A.aY(B.ac9,"A [[token]] is a word piece. The model thinks in pieces, not whole words.")
B.a9E=s(['In 2017, "Attention Is All You Need" gave us the [[transformer]]\u2014the blueprint for modern AI brains.',"Tap each block. [[Embedding]] is a word's address on a map. [[Attention]] decides who to listen to.","The [[feed-forward layer]] looks things up in memory. The [[parameters|parameter]] are the knobs.","GPT-3 had 175 billion knobs across 96 [[layers|layer]]; Llama 3 405B stacks 126. Drag the slider and watch the building grow."],t.s)
B.a7g=new A.aY(B.a9E,"A [[transformer]] is a stack of [[layers|layer]]. Each has [[attention]] (who to listen to) and a [[feed-forward layer]] (look it up in memory).")
B.aeg=s(['You say, "The sky is blue because light scatters." Which word does "scatters" care about? "Light."',"That's [[attention]]\u2014deciding who to listen to. Tap a word and the curved lines show what it's attending to.","It's like a noisy room. You turn your head toward the friend who just said your name. The model has many little listeners like that, called heads."],t.s)
B.a5L=new A.aY(B.aeg,'[[Attention]] lets every word check in with the others. "Blue" looks at "sky"; "scatters" looks at "light."')
B.aja=s(["The model's training job is brutally simple: guess the next [[token]]. One piece of a word at a time.","I'll hide the next word. You guess first, then I show the [[forward pass]] result\u2014an untrained model just shrugs.",'Once trained, it sees "The sky is\u2014" and puts a huge bar on "blue." Inside, every word gets a score; the [[softmax]] turns those scores into chances.'],t.s)
B.a6g=new A.aY(B.aja,"Training is one question, repeated trillions of times: what's the next [[token]]?")
B.akC=s(["How wrong was the guess? That's [[loss]]\u2014the wrongness score. Lower is better.","Drag the probability slider. If the model was 99% sure about the right word, the loss is tiny.","If it was 1% sure, the loss spikes\u2014\u2212log(p). It's like a quiz where getting it wrong stings."],t.s)
B.a5K=new A.aY(B.akC,"[[Loss]] measures how surprised the model is. High loss means very wrong; low loss means confident and right.")
B.acV=s(["The model guessed wrong. [[Backpropagation]] passes the blame backward to every [[parameter]].",'Watch the red pulses travel from the output through every knob. Each gets a tiny arrow: "Turn me a little."',"That arrow is the [[gradient]]. Rumelhart, Hinton, and Williams made [[backpropagation]], the method for finding that arrow, famous in 1986. It's why AI can learn."],t.s)
B.a6p=new A.aY(B.acV,"[[Backpropagation]] figures out which [[parameters|parameter]] caused the mistake and how much to blame each one.")
B.af8=s(["The model now knows which way is downhill. [[Gradient descent]] is walking downhill in the fog, one step at a time.","The [[learning rate]] is your step size. Too small and you crawl; too big and you bounce over the valley.","Adam, the 2014 optimizer, smooths the path. It remembers which way the slope has been pointing, like a skier who knows the hill, and how jumpy each knob's slope has been: a knob whose slope keeps swinging wildly takes smaller steps."],t.s)
B.a5O=new A.aY(B.af8,"[[Gradient descent]] walks downhill. The [[learning rate]] sets the step size; Adam smooths the walk.")
B.abu=s(["Your turn. Here's a real, tiny [[neural network]]: two [[layers|layer]] and just 33 [[parameters|parameter]].","Its job: tell the violet dots in the middle from the cyan dots in the ring. Press Train and watch the [[loss]] curve slide down.","Nudge the [[learning rate]] or reset if it goes wild. It's your brain to train."],t.s)
B.a6d=new A.aY(B.abu,"A [[neural network]] learns by adjusting [[parameters|parameter]] to reduce [[loss]]. Even 33 knobs can find a curved boundary on their own, without anyone drawing it.")
B.a7W=s(["You trained a tiny brain on a few hundred dots. Now imagine doing that trillions of times.","The formula: [[Compute]] \u2248 6 \xd7 [[parameters|parameter]] \xd7 [[tokens|token]]. GPT-3 ran about 3.14 \xd7 10\xb2\xb3 [[FLOP]]s.","Llama 3 405B chewed 15.6 trillion tokens\u2014about 30.84 million H100 [[GPU]]-hours. Even well-tuned, [[GPUs|gpu]] sit at 38\u201343% of peak.","GPT-3 used about 1,287 megawatt-hours\u2014enough power for 120 U.S. homes for a year. Tap the calculator and watch the days pile up."],t.s)
B.a6b=new A.aY(B.a7W,"[[Compute]] is brain-sweat, scaling with [[parameters|parameter]] \xd7 [[tokens|token]] \xd7 6. Training a brain is measured in megawatt-hours of energy, not just hours of time.")
B.a9o=s(["One [[GPU]] is a line cook. A [[datacenter]] is a factory of kitchens.","Data parallel: every kitchen cooks the same recipe on different food. Tensor parallel: one dish split across chefs.","Pipeline parallel: an assembly line. Tap each mode and watch the [[GPUs|gpu]] light up."],t.s)
B.a5M=new A.aY(B.a9o,"[[Parallelism]] is splitting the work\u2014by data, by tensor, or by pipeline. The [[GPUs|gpu]] cook as a team.")
B.a9_=s(["Big training runs don't go smoothly. In a 54-day stretch of Llama 3 405B, there were 466 interruptions.","47 were planned. 419 were unexpected, and 58.7% of those were [[GPU]] problems.","Tap the markers on the timeline. In the demo below, the loss spikes, then the [[checkpoint]]\u2014the save game\u2014kicks in and the model rewinds."],t.s)
B.a5V=new A.aY(B.a9_,"A [[checkpoint]] is a save game. Even 419 unexpected failures didn't stop the model from finishing.")
B.aeR=s(["Along the way, the model takes [[benchmark]] exams. Same standardized questions every time.","Drag the slider through training progress. Watch the wrongness score fall as the model learns.","Then something strange happens at scale. [[Emergent abilities|emergent ability]]\u2014like multi-step arithmetic\u2014switch on."],t.s)
B.a6I=new A.aY(B.aeR,"[[Benchmarks|benchmark]] track progress. At a certain size, [[emergent abilities|emergent ability]] appear\u2014the model can suddenly do things it couldn't before.")
B.afa=s(["The [[base model]] is a well-read parrot. It finishes any sentence, but it doesn't know how to be helpful.","[[Fine-tuning]] is specialist training. Then [[RLHF]]\u2014thumbs up and down\u2014teaches it to answer like a person.","A 1.3B InstructGPT beat the 175B GPT-3\u2014a model over 100\xd7 larger. [[Constitutional AI]] takes the training further: instead of waiting for human votes, the model critiques itself using written principles.","Play the rater. Two answers appear\u2014pick the better one."],t.s)
B.a5P=new A.aY(B.afa,"[[Fine-tuning]] turns a [[base model]] into an assistant. [[RLHF]] and [[Constitutional AI]] teach helpfulness, not just fluency.")
B.anY=s(["[[Red-teaming]] means friendly hackers attack on purpose. They poke the model with tricky [[prompts|prompt]] to see if it slips.","Try it. A tricky request appears: can you spot what could go wrong, and what a careful answer looks like?","Testers also check for bias, dangerous advice, and [[hallucinations|hallucination]], when a model makes a confident wrong guess. Problems found are fixed before release."],t.s)
B.a6D=new A.aY(B.anY,"[[Red-teaming]] finds the cracks before the public does. Testing is part of building a model, not an afterthought.")
B.aeP=s(["Bigger isn't always better. The [[scaling law]] says loss falls smoothly as you add [[parameters|parameter]], data, and [[compute]].","But there's a sweet spot. Chinchilla, a 2022 DeepMind study, showed a 70B model trained on 1.4 trillion tokens beat much bigger models trained on less data.","The optimal ratio is about 20 [[tokens|token]] per [[parameter]]. Drag the slider and watch the curve find its best point."],t.s)
B.a6z=new A.aY(B.aeP,"The [[scaling law]] has a sweet spot. About 20 [[tokens|token]] per [[parameter]] gives the best result for the [[compute]] spent.")
B.ac3=s(["You don't always need the biggest brain. Phi-3-mini is 3.8B [[parameters|parameter]] and runs on a phone.","Llama 3 405B is the giant\u2014405 billion [[parameters|parameter]], 126 [[layers|layer]], a 128,000-token [[context window]].","Toggle the sizes. Watch the radar chart. The giant knows more; the tiny one is faster and cheaper.",'Which [[model]] answers "Why is the sky blue?" Any of them. The big one just has more knowledge to draw on.'],t.s)
B.a5J=new A.aY(B.ac3,"Bigger [[models|model]] know more and reason deeper. Smaller ones are faster, cheaper, and can live on your phone.")
B.aj3=s(["[[Distillation]] is a teacher training a student. A small model learns to imitate a big model's output probabilities.","[[Quantization]] is compressing a photo. The numbers get coarser and the model shrinks.","Tap the slider. At 4 bits per number, even a 65B model can be fine-tuned on a single 48 GB [[GPU]].","[[Mixture of experts]] is a team of specialists. Mixtral 8x7B has 8 experts of about 7B knobs that share some parts, so 46.7B total, but only 12.9B fire per [[token]]. Tap a word and watch the router light up two of the eight experts."],t.s)
B.a71=new A.aY(B.aj3,"You can make a [[model]] cheaper to run with [[distillation]], [[quantization]], or [[Mixture of experts]]. Most of the skill, for much less memory and [[compute]].")
B.anf=s(["Training needs far more memory than just running a model. Every knob drags along a little backpack of extra numbers.","There's the weight itself, its [[gradient]] (which way to turn), and the optimizer's notes about recent steps. Together that's about 16 bytes per [[parameter]].","Slide the model size and watch the memory pile up. Then split the work across more [[GPUs|gpu]] and see each one's share shrink."],t.s)
B.a6N=new A.aY(B.anf,"Training a model needs roughly eight times the memory of just storing it, so the weights, gradients and optimizer notes are split across many GPUs.")
B.a8l=s(["Remember the parts from Section 1? Now let's watch them all work together on one single training step.","A batch of text comes off the storage, the [[GPU]] makes its guesses, measures the [[loss]], and passes the blame backward.","Then thousands of GPUs compare notes over [[NVLink]] and [[InfiniBand]], so every copy of the model learns the same lesson. Step through it."],t.s)
B.a6W=new A.aY(B.a8l,"One training step uses every part of the machine: storage feeds data, HBM feeds the tensor cores, and the network keeps thousands of GPUs in sync.")
B.abw=s(["We built the whole machine. Now for the fun part \u2014 let's type our question and send it in.","Press send. Your question flies in as a [[prompt]], and the machine begins [[inference]] \u2014 doing the job, not going to school."],t.s)
B.a7c=new A.aY(B.abw,"The mind answers by doing inference \u2014 the job it was trained to do \u2014 starting with your prompt as the first piece of input.")
B.akv=s(["Before the brain can think, it chops your words into tiny pieces. That's the [[tokenizer]] \u2014 the word chopper.","Each piece is a [[token]], like a puzzle chip. The model only knows its [[vocabulary]] \u2014 its own dictionary.","Type your own sentence and watch the chips appear in real time, each stamped with a little ID number."],t.s)
B.a6n=new A.aY(B.akv,"The model doesn't read words \u2014 it reads numbered puzzle pieces, and the tokenizer is the translator that chops your sentence into those pieces.")
B.ake=s(["Now each token gets an [[embedding]] \u2014 its address on a map. Close words sit close together, like neighbors on a street.",'Tap any word to see its neighbours. "King" is a short hop from "queen," and "man" is right next to "woman." Distance is meaning.',"Try the trick: the step from man to woman is the same as the step from king to queen, so starting at king and taking the man-to-woman step (king minus man plus woman) lands on queen. The map does the thinking."],t.s)
B.a5Q=new A.aY(B.ake,"Words live on a geometric map where meaning is distance, so the model can do arithmetic with ideas \u2014 that's how it understands relationships.")
B.ami=s(["Here's a tiny [[transformer]] \u2014 the blueprint of modern AI \u2014 shrunk to a single floor, a 12-word vocabulary and 4 numbers per word.","[[Attention]] is the model deciding who to listen to in your sentence. The [[feed-forward layer]] is where it looks things up in memory.","Then it outputs [[logits]] \u2014 raw scores \u2014 and [[softmax]] turns those scores into actual chances. You'll see every number."],t.s)
B.a6R=new A.aY(B.ami,"A transformer figures out which words matter to each other through attention, looks up patterns in memory, and converts raw scores into a probability for the next word.")
B.ajC=s(["The model has a bar chart of every possible next word, ranked by [[softmax]] chances. Now it has to pick one.","Turn up the [[temperature]] \u2014 the creativity dial \u2014 and the bars even out, so unlikely words get a real chance. Turn [[top-p]] down and only the likely words survive.",'Hit "roll the dice" and watch which word it picks. Try a few times \u2014 sometimes it surprises me.'],t.s)
B.a6Z=new A.aY(B.ajC,"The model doesn't pick the best word; it rolls a weighted dice where the odds come from the probabilities, and the temperature and top-p sliders shape that dice.")
B.a7M=s(["The answer grows [[autoregressive]] \u2014 one word at a time. Each new word gets fed back in, and the machine keeps going.","Here's the trick: it keeps a [[KV cache]] \u2014 notes so it doesn't re-read. Like your meeting notes, it doesn't start over from page one.","Toggle the cache off and watch it recompute from scratch. Each token stores about 0.33 megabytes of notes \u2014 128,000 tokens pile up to roughly 40 gigabytes."],t.s)
B.a5H=new A.aY(B.a7M,"The model writes one word, reads it back, writes the next, and keeps a running set of notes so it never has to re-read the conversation from the top.")
B.aj1=s(["You can run a mind at home. Pick a gaming PC, a Mac, or the DGX Spark \u2014 a 1.2-kilogram box with 128 gigabytes of [[unified memory]].","Try [[quantization]] \u2014 compressing a photo \u2014 to shrink the model. Then check: does it fit in the machine's memory?","If it fits, I'll estimate its [[tokens per second]] \u2014 its talking speed. It's roughly [[bandwidth]] divided by model size in bytes.","Fun fact: a median Gemini text prompt uses about 0.24 watt-hours. That's roughly nine seconds of your living-room TV."],t.s)
B.a6m=new A.aY(B.aj1,"At home, how fast a model answers (its inference speed) is mostly about how fast your memory can hand data to the chip \u2014 bandwidth divided by model size \u2014 not about raw brain power.")
B.akQ=s(["You just shrank a mind to a quarter of its size. Now look at a download page: Llama-70B-Q4_K_M, IQ1_S, UD-Q5_K_XL \u2014 it reads like a robot grocery list.","Today we crack those names open, one piece at a time. Every letter tells you something: how many bits each number kept, and what trick protects the rest.","Tap each piece of a filename and I'll translate. Then play with the calculator \u2014 [[Bits per weight]] is the measuring stick for the whole game."],t.s)
B.a6l=new A.aY(B.akQ,"A model filename is a recipe card: the number says how many bits each weight kept, and the letters say which trick is used to keep the model smart with fewer of them.")
B.akW=s(["Zoom out. A frontier model lives in a [[datacenter]] \u2014 a factory for computing \u2014 spread across thousands of [[GPUs|gpu]] on [[NVLink]] express lanes.","Pinch the screen to go from one tiny token to the whole cluster. Watch the token flow through every layer of the stack.","Some models use [[Mixture of experts]] \u2014 a team of specialists where only a few wake for each token. Watch the routing light up."],t.s)
B.a5S=new A.aY(B.akW,"A frontier model is split across many GPUs in a datacenter, serving huge numbers of people at once; in mixture-of-experts models, only a few experts do the math for each token.")
B.a8A=s(["ChatGPT has more than one billion weekly active users. The datacenter handles the flood with [[batching]] \u2014 carpooling.","Slide the user count up. Watch how [[batch]] groups let one GPU serve many people at once, and how each user's [[KV cache]] notes are stored in pages.","That paging trick lets one GPU serve two to four times more requests. Another trick, speculative decoding, lets a small model draft and the big model check: two to three times faster, same output, because checking several words at once costs about the same as writing one."],t.s)
B.a6O=new A.aY(B.a8A,"Datacenters serve millions by carpooling requests into batches and managing each user's memory notes in pages, so the same hardware handles far more conversations at once.")
B.amJ=s(["Text is just one sense. [[Multimodal]] means taking in more than text \u2014 the mind can see and hear, and it can also act by using tools.","Tap the modes. Then watch the [[agent]] loop: think, use a tool, observe, repeat. It's how coding agents like Claude Code write and fix code.","It's a person who thinks, grabs a pen, writes something, reads it back, and tries again. The loop keeps going until the job's done."],t.s)
B.a73=new A.aY(B.amJ,"A multimodal agent doesn't just predict words \u2014 it sees, hears, and acts in a loop of thinking, using tools, and observing results until the task is complete.")
B.aoL=s(["Let's rewind. In 1997, Deep Blue beat Garry Kasparov. In 2012, AlexNet won an image contest on two GPUs and the boom started.","In 2016, AlphaGo made a move with a one-in-ten-thousand chance of being played by a human. In 2017, the [[transformer]] blueprint was born.","By 2022, ChatGPT hit a million users in five days. Tap any milestone on the timeline for the full story \u2014 there's a lot to unpack."],t.s)
B.a61=new A.aY(B.aoL,"From chess and protein folding to coding agents and eighteen-hour engineering sessions, each new design let AI do something the last one couldn't.")
B.aib=s(["Now the mind gets a body. A [[VLA model]] \u2014 eyes, words, and hands in one brain \u2014 sees the room, understands, and moves.","Watch the arm fold laundry. In 2023, RT-2 turned web knowledge into robot actions; in 2024, \u03c00 folded shirts and packed boxes.","By July 2026, Gemini Robotics 2 gives humanoid robots whole-body control. The loop is always see, understand, act, then check that it worked."],t.s)
B.a6L=new A.aY(B.aib,"A VLA model connects vision and language to physical action in a loop \u2014 see, understand, act, check \u2014 turning abstract knowledge into a hand that folds your laundry.")
B.alj=s(["The mind doesn't just chat. AlphaFold released over 200 million protein structures for free, and in 2024 it won a Nobel Prize in Chemistry.","GraphCast makes a ten-day forecast in under a minute, beating the top system on 90% of 1,380 targets. Europe's weather centre put AI forecasts into everyday use in 2025.","GNoME predicted 2.2 million new crystal materials, about 380,000 stable. Over 1,600 AI-enabled medical devices are FDA-authorized, and weather services now use AI forecasts alongside their traditional physics models."],t.s)
B.a6J=new A.aY(B.alj,"The same kind of AI that answers your questions is folding proteins, forecasting storms in under a minute, and designing new materials \u2014 and helping doctors and weather forecasters keep people safe.")
B.aoS=s(["And here it is. The answer assembles token by token: sunlight scatters, blue scatters in all directions, and your eyes see blue.","Look at the journey map \u2014 three sections, real silicon, real math. Your question traveled through all of it to get this sentence.","Hit play for a twenty-second replay. The same kind of math that answered you is folding proteins, forecasting storms, and helping doctors.","And the best part? You asked. You're the one holding the phone, curious and wondering \u2014 the machine is just the path your question travels."],t.s)
B.a6v=new A.aY(B.aoS,"The answer to your question was built by real math in real hardware \u2014 and the same kind of mind that explains the sky is already helping science, medicine, and the world around you.")
B.a6s=new A.aY(B.x3,"The machine is impressive, but the curiosity that sends a question into it \u2014 that spark in a person \u2014 is what makes it matter.")
B.a9Y=s(['I put two piles in front of you \u2014 "Great at" on the left, "Watch out" on the right. Tap any card to flip it over and see a real example of why it belongs there.',"Now the fun part. I'll show you a chatbot bubble with a few sentences about a topic. One of them is a confident little lie. Tap the one you think is made up, and I'll tell you why it was wrong.","Three rounds, three topics. When you finish, remember the habit: check names, numbers, quotes, and links before you trust a confident answer."],t.s)
B.a66=new A.aY(B.a9Y,"A model can sound certain while quietly inventing a detail, so the safe move is to verify the specific facts it gives you.")
B.aoK=s(["I started with the laziest request you can think of \u2014 \"write an email.\" Watch the preview answer: it's fine, but it's also a guess. Now tap the chips around it \u2014 who it's for, the goal, the tone, the length \u2014 and see the answer reshape itself.",'Each chip you toggle nudges the mock answer into a different flavour. You don\'t need to be perfect; you just need to be a little more specific than "write an email."',"On the second card, I've left a small checklist: ask the model to ask **you** questions first, and ask for sources, then check them. Tap through it when you're ready."],t.s)
B.a6E=new A.aY(B.aoK,"The more specific your [[prompt]] is, the less the model has to guess, and the less it will invent to fill the gaps.")
B.a9L=s(["Your message doesn't just vanish. It travels from your phone to a company's servers, and from there it might get folded into training data, read by a human, or simply deleted after a set time. Tap an app's name to see which gates your words pass through.","I've drawn the path for ChatGPT, Gemini, Copilot, Claude, and Meta AI, each with its own menu route to the \"use my chats to improve models\" setting. Pick one and I'll point you to the exact toggle.","Flip the \"training off\" switch and watch that branch of the path go grey. And here's my one rule for every app: never paste passwords, ID numbers, or someone else's private info into a chat."],t.s)
B.a6C=new A.aY(B.a9L,"Where your words end up depends on the app and the settings you can actually change, so it's worth knowing the path before you type.")
B.adL=s(['I\'ve drawn three little comic strips: a "grandchild in trouble" call where the voice sounds just right, a video of a celebrity selling something they never filmed, and an email that looks urgent and official.',"Tap the red flags in each panel \u2014 the pressure to act now, the request for secrecy, the payment by gift card or crypto, the link that looks one letter off. I'll light up each one you find.","After each story, a habit card pops up: agree on a family safe word that no [[voice clone]] could know, and if something sounds urgent, hang up and call back on a number you already know."],t.s)
B.a7_=new A.aY(B.adL,"AI can clone a voice or a face close enough to trick the people you love, so the defence is a small routine, not a bigger screen.")
B.adn=s(['I\'ve lined up eight little scenario cards \u2014 from "ask AI to explain a maths step you\'re stuck on" to "paste the whole essay question and hand in the answer." Sort each one into the pile that fits: "Fine," "Depends \u2014 check the rules," or "Not OK."',"I'll give you a short note on every card you drop, so you can see why it landed where it did. Some answers are genuinely helpful; others quietly do the thinking for you.","When the last card is sorted, I'll leave you three habits: check the rules first, say when you used AI, and remember you're responsible for whatever you hand in."],t.s)
B.a6_=new A.aY(B.adn,'AI is a drafting partner, not a replacement for your own thinking, and the line between "helpful" and "cheating" is the rule you agreed to follow.')
B.af6=s(["I've drawn a wheel of tasks \u2014 write, summarise a long document, make a picture, talk out loud, code, search the web for news, help with a photo of a problem. Tap the one you're curious about.","When you tap, I'll highlight the kind of tool that fits \u2014 a chat assistant, an image generator, a voice mode, a coding assistant, or an AI search \u2014 and name which big apps offer that feature.","I won't rank them. I'll just tell you what to watch out for: an image generator won't answer a question, and a chat assistant without web search doesn't know today's news."],t.s)
B.a5Z=new A.aY(B.af6,"The right tool depends on the shape of the task, not the brand, so matching the job to the tool type is the whole trick.")
B.aeJ=s(["Most big AI companies sell their models in sizes, like coffee: a quick small one, a middle one, and a big slow one that thinks hardest.","Pick a company to see its line-up in its own words: OpenAI's GPT-6 Astra down to Luna, Anthropic's Claude Fable down to Haiku, Google's Gemini 4 Argon down to Flash-Lite.","The biggest can cost a hundred times more per word than the smallest, and it's slower. Then match some everyday jobs to a size."],t.s)
B.a64=new A.aY(B.aeJ,"Start with a small or middle model, and move up only when the answers fall short. The biggest model is for the hardest, longest jobs.")
B.ab_=s(["I've opened a door to a little workbench. On the bench sit a laptop, a gaming PC, and a Mac, each with a \"what fits\" bar showing small, medium, or large models. Tap the one you'd actually use.","I'll show you what you could run on that machine and how it feels \u2014 fast and snappy, or a little slow but still useful. And I'll mention the privacy upside: your words stay in your room.","When you're ready to go deeper \u2014 [[VRAM]], quantized models, the real trade-offs \u2014 there's a big button here that steps you into the Enthusiast Lab."],t.s)
B.a6q=new A.aY(B.ab_,"You can run a real language model on hardware you already own, and the size you can fit is the main thing that shapes how it feels.")
B.ak8=s(["Chatbots learned from a mountain of human writing, and people's habits came along, the unfair ones too. So answers can lean one way without anyone meaning them to.",'Play "Spot the lean": five questions, two answers each. Tap the one that slips in a stereotype. Then see how researchers measure the lean and what you can do about it.'],t.s)
B.a6k=new A.aY(B.ak8,"A model can fill gaps with stereotypes it absorbed from human writing, so watch for answers that add a gender, age or origin you never gave, and keep decisions about people with people.")
B.an8=s(["People ask chatbots about symptoms, taxes and landlords every day. It can really help, as long as you know which job it's good for.","Think of it as a well-read friend: brilliant at explaining and preparing, but it hasn't examined you or read your contract. Then sort eight real-life situations: ask the AI, use it to prepare, or go straight to a person?"],t.s)
B.a6i=new A.aY(B.an8,"Use a chatbot to understand words and prepare questions, but let a qualified person decide anything about your health, your legal case or your money, and never wait on a chatbot in an emergency.")
B.afV=s(["Teachers, editors and bosses all want to know: did a person write this, or an AI? Tools that promise to tell you are everywhere. Can they?",'First, how detectors guess. Then play "Detector roulette": six short texts, you and a detector both guess, and the truth is revealed. Last, what actually works.'],t.s)
B.a6f=new A.aY(B.afV,"AI-text detectors guess from how predictable the wording is, so they wrongly flag plain human writing and miss reworded AI text, and a score should never be treated as proof.")
B.acF=s(["For some people, the AI in this app isn't a toy or a shortcut. It reads their letters, captions their conversations, or speaks for them.","Meet four real people who told their own stories in public: a blind creator, a deaf scientist, a congresswoman and a football player. Then walk through a day and pick the tool that helps at each moment."],t.s)
B.a6F=new A.aY(B.acF,"The same AI that chats can describe the world, caption speech and give people their own voice back, which is why making it accurate and fair matters so much.")
B.an3=s(["Welcome to the Lab's favourite question: will this model fit on my machine, and how fast will it run?","Pick a model, a quant and a context length, then pick your machine. The bar shows weights, [[KV cache]] and overhead against your [[VRAM]]; whatever spills over has to live in system RAM.",'Tap "Show the math" to see every number. The speed is an upper bound: generating a token means reading every active weight once, so memory bandwidth sets the pace.'],t.s)
B.a6y=new A.aY(B.an3,"Memory decides whether a model fits and memory bandwidth decides how fast it talks, so size, quant, context and the type of memory matter more than raw compute at home.")
B.aec=s(["You've downloaded a model. Now you need an engine: the program that actually does the math, and usually serves it to a chat app through a common [[OpenAI-compatible API]].",'Here are the main local engines, each described in its own words. Tap a filter like "I have a Mac" or "Many users" to see which fit, and tap an engine for its file format and hardware.',"The weights are the same numbers whichever engine runs them; what changes is the file format, the hardware it supports and how many people it can serve at once."],t.s)
B.a5U=new A.aY(B.aec,"Pick the engine for your hardware and your use, one person or many, because the same model can run in several engines that differ in formats, speed and convenience.")
B.akH=s(["Every quant trades file size for damage. Here is the real measurement for Llama 3 8B from the llama.cpp project: size on one side, how far the answers drift from the original on the other.","Slide through the quants. The drift is measured as [[KL divergence]]: zero means identical. Notice how slowly it rises down to about 4-5 bits, and how fast it climbs below 3.","Switch on the importance matrix to see the same sizes do less damage. Then look at the surprise underneath: the newer, better model suffers more from the same squeeze."],t.s)
B.a78=new A.aY(B.akH,"Quants around 4 to 6 bits keep most of a model's answers unchanged, an importance matrix makes the same size better, and newer models can be more sensitive than older ones.")
B.abP=s(['Every token you add leaves notes behind: a key and a value in every single layer. Tap "Next token" and watch the pile grow.',"That pile is the [[KV cache]]. Now try real models: slide the context up, and see how many gigabytes the notes take next to the weights themselves.","Two tricks keep it in check. Grouped-query attention lets several heads share one key and value (flip the switch to see the size without it), and the cache itself can be stored in 8 or 4 bits."],t.s)
B.a65=new A.aY(B.abP,"Context costs memory because every token keeps a key and a value in every layer, so long chats need room for the KV cache on top of the model.")
B.adq=s(['A [[mixture-of-experts|mixture of experts]] model has many expert blocks in every layer, but a router sends each token to only a few. Tap "Next token" and watch different experts light up.',"The experts that sit idle are never read for that token. So the experts can live in cheap system RAM, while the parts every token needs stay on the GPU.","Slide how many layers keep their experts on the GPU, and watch the VRAM, the RAM and the speed limit change. The command line underneath uses llama.cpp's real options."],t.s)
B.a6c=new A.aY(B.adq,"Because a mixture-of-experts model reads only a few experts per token, its experts can sit in system RAM, which is how huge models run on one gaming GPU.")
B.abm=s(["A big model writes one token per pass, and every pass reads all its weights. What if a small, fast model guessed a few tokens ahead, and the big one just checked them?","Run a few rounds. Green guesses were right and come for free; at the first wrong one the big model writes its own token instead, so the result is exactly what it would have written alone.","The second card has the formula from the paper. Play with the acceptance rate and the number of drafted tokens to see when guessing ahead pays off."],t.s)
B.a7a=new A.aY(B.abm,"Speculative decoding lets a cheap guesser propose tokens that the big model checks in one pass, so you get the same answer faster whenever the guesses are usually right.")
B.amB=s(["The engine speaks an [[OpenAI-compatible API]]. Everything you actually talk to \u2014 the chat box, the document search, the agent loop \u2014 sits on top of it.","Tap each box in the diagram to see what it does. Try the RAG mini-demo: a question, six document chunks, and a search that highlights the two closest.","The agent loop animates: plan, call a tool, read the result, plan again."],t.s)
B.a6t=new A.aY(B.amB,"The model sits behind an API, and the [[RAG]] pipeline, the [[MCP]] tool layer, and the agent loop are all front-ends that shape what the model sees and does.")
B.alv=s(["Theory is nice; here is a real home lab: the one this app was built on, with names left out. A workstation with two 24 GB GPUs and lots of RAM, and a small server with one 16 GB GPU.","Step through what tuning actually bought. The surprise: the biggest wins came from where the experts live and how fast the RAM is, not from the GPUs.","Then the honest part: tap each thing that went wrong, from a 10-hour runaway stress test to a power cut that made the GPUs vanish, and what each one taught."],t.s)
B.a6M=new A.aY(B.alv,"On a home rig the bottleneck is usually memory, and the risks are heat and power, so measure before you tune and never leave a stress test unwatched.")
B.anJ=s(["One [[GPU]] too small? Use two. But there are two very different ways to share a model, and only one of them makes it faster.","Flip between them. A [[layer split]] is an assembly line: each card runs some floors of the building in turn. A [[tensor split]] is two people carrying one sofa: both work on every layer, but they must keep talking.","Then play \"Split it\": put a 70B model's 80 layers on two 24 GB cards. Overfill a card and it won't load; leave layers in RAM and the speed drops hard."],t.s)
B.a6G=new A.aY(B.anJ,"Splitting by layers mainly lets a bigger model fit; splitting each layer can be faster, but only when the cards can talk quickly, and a model that fits on one card usually beats both.")
B.aaS=s(["A model server at home is a computer that does what it's told. The job is making sure only **you** get to tell it, and that what it reads can't give it orders.",'Play "Find the hole": tap the nine numbered spots in this home and call each one risky or fine. Several come straight from a real phone bridge built for a home AI box, bugs included.',"Then keep the six rules. None of them costs money; most are the defaults you just shouldn't undo."],t.s)
B.a5Y=new A.aY(B.aaS,"Keep the server off the open internet, lock it with a key, give [[agents|agent]] only the power they need, and treat everything they read as data rather than orders.")
B.abc=s(["A voice assistant is two extra parts around the chat [[model]]: ears that turn speech into text, and a mouth that turns text back into speech. Both run fine at home.","First the ears. Tap through Whisper's six sizes and watch memory and speed trade places. Then the mouth: your Windows PC already has voices, and a free download makes them sound natural.","Last, pick a setup for four everyday jobs, from reading notes aloud to transcribing a meeting on a laptop with no graphics card."],t.s)
B.a5R=new A.aY(B.abc,"Pick the smallest speech model that's accurate enough for the job, and remember that Windows and open tools like Whisper and Piper already cover most home uses for free, offline.")
B.abI=s(["You want a model to do something it doesn't do yet. There are three ways to get there, and they cost very different amounts of effort.","Answer two questions and watch the path light up: [[RAG]] when it's missing knowledge, [[fine-tuning]] when it needs a new habit every time, a plain prompt for everything else. Or tap a scenario.","Then look at the ladder of costs. Most problems are solved on the first rung."],t.s)
B.a6a=new A.aY(B.abI,"Missing knowledge calls for retrieval, a missing habit calls for fine-tuning, and everything else starts with a better prompt.")
B.amn=s(["Fine-tuning every weight of a big model needs a datacenter. [[LoRA]] freezes the original weights and trains a tiny patch instead.","The patch is two thin matrices whose product has the same shape as the big one. Slide the rank and watch how few numbers you actually train.","Then compare the memory to fine-tune an 8B model: full fine-tuning, LoRA, and QLoRA, which also stores the frozen base in 4 bits."],t.s)
B.a77=new A.aY(B.amn,"LoRA trains a small low-rank patch on top of frozen weights, and QLoRA shrinks the frozen base to 4 bits, which brings fine-tuning down to a single consumer GPU.")
B.aj_=s(["A [[chat template]] stacks system, user, and assistant turns around special [[token]] markers \u2014 the model only ever sees this structure.","Tap the coloured blocks to reveal the raw text underneath, then look at a real JSONL row to see how a training example is stored.","Now play the mini-game: three rows are on screen, and one of them is a bad example. Spot it."],t.s)
B.a7h=new A.aY(B.aj_,"The model learns from examples wrapped in a strict template, and the quality of those examples shapes what it can actually do.")
B.agL=s(['Two people fine-tuned the same base model: one for maths, one for code. Each change is an arrow pointing away from the base, a "task vector".',"Pick a way to merge: average them, add them with task arithmetic, walk along the arc with SLERP, or subtract one to remove a behaviour.","Real merges also clean up first. Switch between TIES and DARE to see how they keep the important changes and drop the noise."],t.s)
B.a6H=new A.aY(B.agL,"Models fine-tuned from the same base can be merged by combining their task vectors, and methods like TIES and DARE stop the changes from cancelling each other out.")
B.anF=s(['Inside a model, ideas like "cheerful" turn out to be directions. Feed it cheerful and gloomy sentences and the two groups land in different places: tap to find the arrow between them.',"Push the model's thinking along that arrow while it writes, and its tone changes. Try the knob. This is a control vector, added at run time and easy to take away.",'Abliteration does the same to the "refusal" direction, but cuts it out of the weights for good. Read what that costs before you try it.'],t.s)
B.a6w=new A.aY(B.anF,"Behaviours can show up as directions inside a model, so they can be turned up, down or removed, and every such change has costs in quality and safety.")
B.akm=s(["Want a chatbot that answers from **your** notes? That's [[RAG]]: search your documents first, then let the [[model]] answer from what it found, with sources.","Step through the six parts with the real settings of a helper built at home for a radio hobby, from 1,754 files. Then look at where one answer's 65 seconds actually went.","Last, tune it yourself. Each round shows the helper as it was; pick the change you'd make, and see what was measured when it was really tried."],t.s)
B.a6j=new A.aY(B.akm,"A notes helper is search plus a model, and the slow or wrong parts are usually in the search, so measure with a fixed set of test questions before and after every change.")
B.ac2=s(['When a chatbot "checks the weather", it never touches the internet itself. It writes a small block of JSON, stops, and lets the app do the work. Let\'s open that up.',"Step through one real round trip: the tool menu the [[model]] sees, the call it writes, the result that comes back. Then meet [[MCP]], the shared plug that lets any app use any tool server.","Then write the calls yourself. Pick the right tool and arguments for five requests; a wrong one shows you exactly the error the model would get back."],t.s)
B.a6o=new A.aY(B.ac2,"Tools work because the model writes a structured request and the app runs it, so clear tool descriptions, strict argument schemas and a human check on risky actions matter more than the model's size.")
B.aoF=s(['What is a [[model]] actually "thinking" when it reads your words? Interpretability researchers build something like a brain scan to find out.','First problem: single neurons are messy, each doing several jobs at once. Untangle one into cleaner "features". Then guess which sentences light up a real feature found inside Claude: the Golden Gate Bridge.',"Last, turn that feature up and meet Golden Gate Claude, a model that couldn't stop talking about one bridge."],t.s)
B.a6S=new A.aY(B.aoF,"Models store ideas as patterns spread over many neurons, and tools like sparse autoencoders can pull out readable features that we can watch and even turn up or down.")
B.aoZ=s(["Research papers look scary, but they all share the same skeleton. Tap a part of the page to see what it is for and how long to spend on it.","Nobody reads a paper once from top to bottom. Switch between the three passes: skim, read, go deep. Most papers only need the first.","Results tables can mislead. Keep the red flags in mind before you believe a headline number."],t.s)
B.a6K=new A.aY(B.aoZ,"Read a paper in passes, starting with the abstract, the figures and the conclusion, and save the maths for the few papers you really need.")
B.al8=s(["Here is the shelf: the papers behind the tools you use, from word vectors to reasoning models, grouped by era.","Tap one for its one idea, a plain summary, where you meet it today and what to read inside. Then check yourself with three questions, and open the real paper: every one has an offline copy.","Papers you finish get a tick. One a week and you'll know the field better than most people who talk about it."],t.s)
B.a5G=new A.aY(B.al8,"A handful of papers explain most of what today's tools do, and each one can be understood from its one idea before you read a single equation.")
B.aiB=s(["This track is dated on purpose: it shows the field as of October 2026, and every card says where it came from.","Two shelves: closed frontier models you use through an app or an API, and open-weight models anyone can download. Filter them, and tap a card for its details and source.","Notice the open shelf: giant [[mixture-of-experts|mixture of experts]] models next to small ones that run on a single good GPU."],t.s)
B.a6A=new A.aY(B.aiB,"The frontier is held by a handful of closed models, while open-weight models, mostly mixtures of experts, follow close behind and can be run by anyone.")
B.aa_=s(["The chips that train and serve today's models, next to the machines on a desk. Switch between memory and bandwidth.","The bars use a log scale: every step is ten times more. A datacenter GPU reads its memory tens of times faster than a desk machine, while a big Mac can hold more.","Remember the Lab rule: memory decides what fits, bandwidth decides how fast it talks."],t.s)
B.a7b=new A.aY(B.aa_,"Datacenter chips win on memory bandwidth by a wide margin, while desk machines with big unified memory can hold large models but run them more slowly.")
B.alo=s(["Six tricks show up in almost every big release this year. Tap each one: what it is, where you meet it, and which paper on the shelf started it.","None of them is magic. Each is an idea from the papers you can read in this Lab, scaled up."],t.s)
B.a63=new A.aY(B.alo,"Today's models combine reasoning before answering, mixtures of experts, very long context, guess-ahead decoding, fewer bits and tool use, and each idea traces back to a paper.")
B.aaI=s(["Leaderboards are where model news starts. Here is a made-up arena ranking; it looks like a clear winner.","Now switch on the uncertainty. When the ranges overlap, the order is noise: those models are tied.","Then read the five traps. A good habit: treat any single number with suspicion, and test the top few on your own tasks."],t.s)
B.a6Q=new A.aY(B.aaI,"A leaderboard rank means little without its uncertainty and the test behind it, so check for overlap, contamination and saturation, then try models on your own work.")
B.aiW=s(["Here is the year so far, June to October 2026, as a timeline. Every entry has a source: tap one to see it.","Open-weight giants, a new flagship from every big lab, and million-token context almost everywhere. By the next version of this app, the list will have grown."],t.s)
B.a6T=new A.aY(B.aiW,"The field moves in months, not years, so date what you know and check the source before you repeat it.")
B.alA=s(["Behind every chatbot sits a tower of companies. Tap a layer to see who works there, from the chip factories at the bottom to the apps at the top.","Two surprises for most people: the company that designs a chip usually doesn't make it, and the biggest clouds design their own AI chips too."],t.s)
B.a6B=new A.aY(B.alA,"The AI industry is a stack, chips, datacenters, models, tools and apps, and each layer depends on the one beneath it.")
B.aaY=s(["You don't need to be a researcher to work in AI. Tap where you come from, and I'll show you the roles closest to what you already know.","Each card says what the job really involves and what to learn first. Most of it you can practise with the tools in this Lab."],t.s)
B.a75=new A.aY(B.aaY,"Most jobs around AI build on a skill you already have, plus hands-on time with real models and tools.")
B.acE=s(["Look at that number: one corner of one research site, one week. Nobody keeps up with everything, and nobody needs to.","Tick the habits that fit your week: a curated list instead of the flood, your engine's release notes, model cards over headlines, one paper a month.","And when something sounds big, try it yourself before you believe it."],t.s)
B.a76=new A.aY(B.acE,"Keeping up means good filters and a small routine, not reading everything, and testing new things yourself before trusting the hype.")
B.akc=s(["Some assistants now **do** things: book, buy, fill in forms, send messages. That's an [[agent]], and it's like giving a house-sitter your key.","First watch the loop every agent runs: plan, act, read the result, repeat. The reading step is where trouble sneaks in.","Then be the gatekeeper. An agent booking your trip asks ten times; allow it or stop and check, and see what each choice means."],t.s)
B.a6e=new A.aY(B.akc,"An agent is only as safe as the permissions you give it, so allow what matches your task and personally check anything with money, consent, your name or orders that came from a page instead of you.")
B.anQ=s(['Newer assistants can "think" before they answer. It sounds like magic, but it\'s a very human trick: working it out on paper instead of in your head.','See what happened when researchers let models write their steps first. Then play "Think or blurt?": for eight everyday questions, decide whether thinking is worth the wait.'],t.s)
B.a5I=new A.aY(B.anQ,"Thinking first helps on puzzles, plans and anything with several steps, but it costs time, so a quick answer is better for simple facts and short writing.")
B.amk=s(['Ever had a chatbot forget something you said an hour ago, then "remember" your name in a brand-new chat? Those are two different kinds of memory.',"First the notepad it can see, the [[context window]]. Send the messages one by one and watch the oldest slide off, then guess which details it can still use.","Then switch the memory feature on and off and start a new chat. Last, see where each big app lets you read, edit or wipe what it keeps."],t.s)
B.a6P=new A.aY(B.amk,"A chatbot sees only what fits in its context window, while the memory feature keeps a small file of notes about you across chats, which you can view, edit and switch off.")
B.aet=s(["Talking to an assistant feels like one thing, but there are two very different machines behind it.","The classic one is a relay of interpreters: ears turn your speech into text, the chat [[model]] answers in text, and a mouth reads it out. The newer one is a single model that hears and speaks. Flip between them.","Then test the relay: which parts of what you say actually reach the brain, and which are lost on the way?"],t.s)
B.a68=new A.aY(B.aet,"A voice relay turns your speech into plain text, so tone and background are lost, while newer one-model voice modes hear sound directly and reply about as fast as a person.")
B.a7K=s(["Does asking a chatbot burn a lot of power? The honest answer needs things you already know: a light bulb, an hour of TV, your home, and the whole country.","First, line up nine things from the least energy to the most. Then climb the zoom-out ladder: every step is a thousand times bigger, from one question all the way to all the energy the United States uses in a year.","Last, see where the tiny numbers add up, and read the small print on who measured what."],t.s)
B.a6Y=new A.aY(B.a7K,"One question uses about as much electricity as a few seconds of TV, but billions of them plus training add up, and data centres have grown from about 2% to over 4% of U.S. electricity.")
B.aeZ=s(["Image generators don't paint and they don't paste photos together. They start with pure static and clean it up, one small step at a time.","Walk through it in five short panels: how pictures are turned into static for training, how the model learns to undo one step, how your words steer it, then run the denoiser yourself.","Last, the tags makers add so you can tell AI pictures apart, and why they're not a lie detector."],t.s)
B.a70=new A.aY(B.aeZ,"An image generator turns random static into a picture by removing a little noise at a time, steered by your words at every step, and the seed decides which picture you get.")
