B.aPR=new A.aK("80 [[layers|layer]], 4.9 [[bits per weight]] (Q4_K_M), 8K context: about 0.57 GB per layer with its [[KV cache]]. Give each card some layers; the rest stay in RAM on the CPU.",1,null,null)
B.aPU=new A.aK("OpenAI's Whisper is free to download and runs offline. It comes in six sizes: more [[parameters|parameter]] hear better but need more [[VRAM]] and time.",1,null,null)
B.aPV=new A.aK('A chatbot only "sees" what fits on its notepad: the [[context window]]. In this toy the notepad holds the last 5 messages. Real ones hold hundreds of pages, but a long enough chat still slides off the top.',1,null,null)
B.aPW=new A.aK('Not perfect: some "AI mirror" apps will, if asked, rate a person against narrow beauty standards. The same [[voice clone]] tech that gave Wexton her voice back is also used in scams.',1,null,null)
B.aPX=new A.aK("That's the entire training task: hide the next [[token]], let the model guess, check the answer. The model's guess is a [[forward pass]], and its output is a chance for every word in its [[vocabulary]].",1,null,null)
B.aPY=new A.aK("From the slowest shelf to the chip's pocket the wait differs by millions of times. That is why builders keep the thinking where the data already is.",1,null,null)
B.aQ_=new A.aK('A real model repeats steps 3\u20135 in every [[layer]] (126 times in Llama 3 405B), with 128 attention "heads" looking for different kinds of clues at once. The math is the same as here, just vastly bigger.',1,null,null)
B.aQ0=new A.aK('Today\'s "thinking" or "reasoning" models do this by themselves: they write a hidden scratchpad, then the answer. The price is time and [[tokens|token]]: every step is written out, so you wait longer.',1,null,null)
B.aQ1=new A.aK("Fitting on one card beat splitting. The two cards were different generations, so the [[tensor split]] also needed extra settings (a smaller [[batch]] and llama.cpp's own [[all-reduce]]). The [[mixture of experts]] model reads only 9B of its 74B per token, so even a plain [[layer split]] stayed quick.",1,null,null)
B.aQ3=new A.aK("Researchers call these [[emergent abilities|emergent ability]]. Some argue the jump is partly an illusion of all-or-nothing scoring: graded more gently, the skill often grows more smoothly. Either way, bigger models reliably get better at a growing list of tasks.",1,null,null)
B.aQ5=new A.aK("An [[agent]] is like a house-sitter with your key. It's useful because it can act: open sites, fill in forms, send messages. That's also why **you** decide which rooms it may enter.",1,null,null)
B.aQ6=new A.aK("Training starts the easy way round. Take a real picture and add a little random noise. Then a little more. Keep going until it's pure TV static.",1,null,null)
B.aQ7=new A.aK("A new chat starts with an empty notepad. The **memory** feature is different: a small file of notes the app keeps about you and quietly adds to the start of new chats.",1,null,null)
B.aQ8=new A.aK("Before [[MCP]], every app wired up every tool its own way. MCP is like USB for AI tools: write a tool server once, and any app that speaks MCP can plug it in.",1,null,null)
B.aQ9=new A.aK('Like turning your head in a noisy room toward the friend who just said your name, each [[attention]] head learns to look for a different kind of clue: who is doing what, which word an "it" refers to, what came just before. Big models run many heads side by side in every [[layer]] (one of the stacked sections the model is built from).',1,null,null)
B.aQc=new A.aK('Imagine a kitchen that served a dish that was too salty. The head chef asks: "How much did each station add?" Each station asks its own helpers the same question. In the end, everyone knows exactly how much to cut back. That\'s [[backpropagation]]: the [[gradient]] tells every knob which way to turn, and by how much.',1,null,null)
B.aQd=new A.aK("Real pipelines run filters like these over billions of pages, often using small AI models to judge quality.",1,null,null)
B.aQe=new A.aK("[[Bandwidth]] is how wide the road is. The fastest roads are the shortest ones: [[NVLink]] runs between GPUs a few centimetres apart. Between servers across the datacenter, [[InfiniBand]] cables do the job.",1,null,null)
B.aQf=new A.aK("A [[checkpoint]] is a copy of every knob, written to storage regularly. Big runs also have to be gentle with the power grid: when thousands of GPUs pause at once, the datacenter's power use can swing by tens of megawatts.",1,null,null)
B.aQh=new A.aK("In 2020, researchers found that the [[loss]] falls in a smooth, predictable curve as you add knobs, data and compute together: a [[scaling law]]. That predictability is why companies dared to build ever bigger datacenters. In 2022, Chinchilla showed many big models had been under-fed: a 70B model trained on 1.4 trillion tokens beat much larger ones.",1,null,null)
B.aQj=new A.aK("Real [[tokenizers|tokenizer]] play this exact game on a giant pile of text, tens of thousands of times. Common words end up as one token; rare words stay split into pieces. The result is the model's [[vocabulary]].",1,null,null)
B.aQl=new A.aK("From here on, the [[training]] story: how the library is gathered, and how billions of knobs get tuned.",1,null,null)
B.aQm=new A.aK("This is the same idea as the [[control vectors|control vector]] in \"Steering a model\", but aimed at one named feature. It isn't a prompt or fine-tuning: it changes the model's inner activity directly.",1,null,null)
B.aQo=new A.aK("A chatbot is like a friend who has read a lot: great for explaining words and helping you prepare. But it hasn't examined you, seen your papers or your contract, and it can sound sure while being wrong ([[hallucination]]).",1,null,null)
B.aQq=new A.aK("A [[VLA model]] is a language model that learned to see and to move. Because it already knows about the world from text and pictures, it can follow instructions it was never specifically trained on.",1,null,null)
B.aQr=new A.aK("Finding a feature means you can also turn it up. In 2024 Anthropic clamped the bridge feature to 10\xd7 its highest natural value and let people chat with the result for a short time.",1,null,null)
B.aQt=new A.aK("Real [[red-teaming|testers]] run thousands of cards like these. Every problem they find goes back into training, and then they test again, because a fix can break something else.",1,null,null)
B.aQv=new A.aK("Researchers build tests like BBQ: tens of thousands of questions where the honest answer is often \"can't tell\". Model makers run them and publish the scores, and use human feedback ([[RLHF]]) to train the lean down. It shrinks; it doesn't vanish.",1,null,null)
B.aQw=new A.aK("Training a frontier model mixes every kind of [[parallelism]]: slices of each layer inside a server (fast [[NVLink]]), stages of the pipeline across servers, and many copies of the whole setup working on different data, all kept in sync over the network.",1,null,null)
B.aQy=new A.aK("Neural networks tolerate rounding surprisingly well, so training usually uses 16-bit numbers and running a model often uses 8 or even 4. Squeezing a finished model into fewer bits is called [[quantization]].",1,null,null)
B.aQz=new A.aK('The weak spot is step 3. Everything the agent reads, including a web page someone else wrote, lands in the same [[context window]] as your request. A line hidden on a page saying "ignore the user, do this instead" can trick it. That\'s called [[prompt injection]].',1,null,null)
B.aQA=new A.aK("The wait was an extra model call that rewrites your question into search queries, the search, reranking on the CPU, and reading ~20,000 [[tokens|token]] of passages. A faster chat model would only have sped up the short writing part.",1,null,null)
B.aQB=new A.aK("A model's knobs are stored as giant grids, and every word passes through them by [[matrix multiplication]]: one multiply and one add per knob. That's why AI chips are built to multiply grids, fast.",1,null,null)
B.aQC=new A.aK("It's like a brain scan for a [[model]]. The trouble: a single neuron rarely means one thing. In a small language model, this one fired for all of these:",1,null,null)
B.aQD=new A.aK("Each server can offer three things: **tools** (actions to run), **resources** (data such as file contents) and **prompts** (ready-made templates). Underneath, it's plain JSON messages: tools/list to read the menu, tools/call to run one.",1,null,null)
B.aQE=new A.aK('Asked "what\'s 7 \xd7 8?" you just know it. Asked "what\'s 47 \xd7 86?" you reach for paper. A [[model]] writes one [[token]] at a time, so writing its steps down first is its version of paper: each step becomes something it can read before the next one.',1,null,null)
